mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote:
> Digital's stock is always fluctuating up and down on that scale over that
> time period - that's the whole problem, it just keeps going up and
> down without ever taking off.  That's because the company isn't growing and
> all you're seeing is trends in investor speculation (and lately a bit of
> price inflation due to the companies own stock buy back program).

I tend to agree. I think that Digital gives off a good signal and it
gives hopes o investors, and the following week, it admits it had been
loosing 
X millions in Y division and sells it off.

If I were a serious investor who does research that goes above just the
numbers in the annual reports, my only recommendation for DEC as a long
term investment would be as a gamble of a takeover target.

Digital may become (or already be) technically profitable, but so is the
kid selling lemonade on the driveway. And the shrunken Digital will not
increase its true value for as long as it has PROVEN that it is the end
of its downsizing.

Palmer may state it is the end of the downsizing/selling off. But in a
few months, when it is revealed that another division has not been able
to invest enough money to keep up with the rest of the industry and stay
profitable, I won't be surprised when it gets sold off. 

Digital couldn't invest enough to make its growing disk business
profitable ENOUGH, so it sold it.

Digital couldn'y invest enough to make its printer business profitable
enough, so it sold it.

Digital couldn't sell enough chips to make its FAB profitable (far from
it), so it sold it.

and the list goes on.

Meanwhile, plenty of other companies have grown and succeeded in those
very same areas.

I am not convinced that Mr Palmer has fixed Digital. It only cut off
limbs that were hurting. And until I am convinced that Palmer has
effectively made Digital a good marketing and sales organisation in this
era of hype and marketing, and until I see Digital products priced
competitively in mail order catalogs, I will remain unconvinced that
Palmer was able to really fix the company.

So, until then, I think it is only a question of time before another
division starts to hurt because the underying endemic problem hasn't
been solved. As more and more of DEC's products start to feel the heat
from commodity-pricing competition, more and more of DEC's divisions
will start to hurt.

Sorry, Palmer may say this is it for divestiture, but I am not
convinced.



