As an Aspen reseller, I can say that my customers have had no complaints
about the products that I had shipped to them. The warm and fuzzies of
Lucent warranty support is good but, the stuff nowadays simply doesn't fail
if it's assembled carefully (which obviously Aspen does).

In article <347C2989.8ACD969A@lucent.com>, gerhart@lucent.com wrote:

> ed notari wrote:
> 
> > I am seriously considering purchasing an Alpha Workstaion clone from
> > either ASPEN SYSTEMS or POLYWELL.  As I do not have any experience with
> > either of these vendors and I do not know anyone who does (nor do I know
> > anybody who knows anybody)I was hoping that readers of this news group
> > could pass along their thoughts.
> 
> I have ordered and received alpha clones from Enorex and Aspen Systems.
> 
> Of Enorex:
> Enorex managed to screw up my order because we wanted 128Mb RAM
> and their standard config was only 64Mb. The result was of 5 machines
> ordered at the same time, I received one group of 3 with the proper
> configuration (but two with ISA bus ethernet and one with PCI ethernet).
> The second group of 2 machines did not come and I called only to find out
> that they didn't seem to know what they had shipped me. After I gave them
> the serial numbers for the machines that I already received, they told me
> it would be weeks before I would get the other two machines. After
> some discussion, I opted to receive the second batch with 64Mb RAM
> with a promise to get 128Mb RAM sets when they were available to
> upgrade the machines myself. When the machines were received, one
> came packed with the 128Mb upgrade ... the other one was a mess. They
> did not bolt down the hard disk and the interior of the machine was
> destroyed by the drive bouncing around during shipping. We exchanged the
> damaged box for an undamaged one and NEVER did get the second 128Mb
> memory upgrade. (I upgraded it myself using some memory I had on hand.)
> When I called Enorex up after opening the machine with the unbolted
> drive, they said that they were ISO 9000 certified and that they had a
> "shake" test as part of their production process. They could not believe
> that they would have shipped a machine in this condition. Needless to say,
> I no longer purchase machines from these guys. They may have cleaned up
> their act by now, but I need a supplier that I can trust if I am going to be
> 
> shipping product on their platform.
> 
> On Aspen:
> 
> Aspen was the second vender selected. (And probably should have
> gotten the business in the first place!) We have ordered ~12 machines
> for our lab and prototype field systems and all have been received in
> good working order. (I think that we have had one bad CDROM
> drive that was promptly replaced.) Aspen has a rack mount configuration
> that allows us to use a higher density rack solution to improve the
> foot print for our customers. They also contract for on-site maintenance
> through the Lucent Systems support organization, so [to the customer], the
> system is installed and maintained by Lucent. Personally, I feel that
> Aspen will be more able to handle our needs as we take our product into
> full production. Aspen has experience in supporting this kind of OEM/VAR
> relationship. I get the feeling that Enorex is selling to a final end-user
> market and gets alot of 1-2 box orders.
> 
> These are my opinions and personal experiences. I do not speak for
> Lucent.
> 
> --
> ----
