> quaking in their boots. There probably won't be SPARC versions of them
> any time soon, either, but I can't see the MIS types writing off
> Solaris because of it.
>

There is a tiny difference. Solaris has 14,000 ish native applications. The native is
importantnative means that the software vendor ported to the Solaris platform and supports the port

to Solaris to the best of their ability. Non native applications running on NT/Alpha mean the
opposite, namely that the software vendor has not ported to NT/Alpha and does not support
the Alpha platform. The does not support bit is the critical point to MIS departments because
it means quite simply they cannot deploy that application on NT/Alpha systems.

Of course Solaris does not run Microsoft Puzzle nor did it support Bob (thankfully) but as you
imply this is not what MIS departments are interested in when they buy servers. They are
interested in things like databases, middleware, managment software etc. One customer
I deal with recently junked a NT/Alpha system in favour of a NT/Intel system reluctantly
because one of the two middle ware products they use was not native on Alpha. They
has reliability problems with it which the software vendor disowned because their software
was running using FX!32.

The reliability problems are the same on the NT/Intel native environment but they can at least
register a support call and expect a patch.

Regards


Andrew Harrison
Senior Consultant SunUK






