jeong wung kee  <youyou@soback.kornet.nm.kr> wrote:

>Now, we need to know how much power must be reserved in UPS for
>DEC-SAFE.
>I have checked DEC technical summary to find that heat dissipation
>of 4100 is 2700 watts.   Then I have checked http://www.digital.com/
>alphaserver/alphasrv4100/as4000_pb.html to find that power supply
>of cabinet style requires 450 watts.
>Then our DEC supplier said it takes 6000 watts !!!
>
>I am not sure what is right -- 450 watts, 2700 watts or 6000 watts ?
>
>I would appreciate if anyone can give me the corrrect information.

Two points:

1.  There's a difference between the the power consumption stated
    on the box (or the specs) and the actual power drawn.

2.  What you really want to base UPS decisions on is not average power,
    but on the maximum RMS AC current draw.  UPS's, you'll note, are rated in
    Volt*Amps, not watts.  Go out, spend $20-$30 for a clamp-on
    AC Ammeter, and figure out what your equipment is really drawing
    at power on, at idle, and at "heavy use" (i.e. disk and tape drives
    all heavily seeking).  *This* is the figure you need to make your UPS
    decisions on.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



