In article <65e73p$oe4$1@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu>,
Andrew Kieschnick  <andrewk@mail.utexas.edu> wrote:
>Thats not the right boot image for a 21164pc - the pc164.img boot image 
>is for a 21164 on a pc164 motherboard. Redhat doesn't seem to have any
>21164pc boot images on their ftp site currently, but you can get some from
>gatekeeper.dec.com in /pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels - try
>milo-sx164-31oct-2030+ for milo and sx164-31oct-2030+.gz for a kernel.

Thank you.  That information got me started in the right direction.  I was
confused by the existence of a PC164 and PC164LX image in a different
directory.  I was expecting to see a file called PC164SX. I now 
have Linux 2.0.30 running on my 21164PC system.
 
I had to get past many obstacles before I finally got the system running. 
I first purchased a 2.1GB Western Digital Caviar hard disk and installed it 
in my computer with its jumper set to the slave mode.  I then purchased a 
set of Red Hat 4.2 PowerTools CDs for about $20 which contained a 
distribution for the Alpha.  I eventually ended up downloading the 
ramdisk41 Red Hat floppy disk image and the Oct 16 versions of the
sx164 MILO and sx164 kernel floppy disk images from the same directory 
as the Oct 31 versions from gatekeeper.  The Oct 31 kernel file is
apparently not a floppy disk image file, and that seems to complicate
the installation of Red Hat Linux.  I added Linux as a boot option in 
the AlphaBIOS setup screen so that it would run linload.exe from the MILO 
disk on the A: drive.  After starting up the MILO disk, I had to use the 
command:
 
     boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1 prompt_ramdisk=1

I then inserted the kernel disk and then the ramdisk41 floppy in succession. 
I discovered from a search on Digital's AltaVista site that Red Hat 
apparently requires a /home and /usr/local partition in addition to the 
root partition for the installation to complete normally.  The intent of 
the /home and /usr/local partitions seems to be to allow you to retain user 
files and programs between reinstallations of Linux.  I know from the 
AltaVista search and now from my own experience that if you do not have the 
/home partition, then the installation of Linux will fail with a,
 
     Mount failed: Invalid argument
 
error message.  I partitioned the 2.1GB hard drive as three primary 
partitions, 512MB for /home, 512MB for /usr/local, and the rest for the 
root.  (In one attempt, when I tried to use a swap partition, my Alpha 
system apparently soon spiralled down into swapping death.)  After the Red 
Hat installation, the system reboots.  I had to use the kernel floppy 
disk one more time before the kernel was activated on the hard disk root 
directory:
 
     boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hdd3
 
/dev/hdd3 is the third partition of the hard disk on which I installed the 
root partition.  After I got Linux installed, I upgraded the MILO file on 
the floppy disk and the vmlinux.gz kernel file on the hard disk's root 
directory with the Oct 31 versions from gatekeepr.  /dev/hda5 is a small 
MSDOS FAT partition that came with Windows NT on the hard disk that came 
with my computer.  It is accessed as drive D: from Windows NT.  On this 
partition, I now have linload.exe and the Oct 31 MILO for faster boot-ups 
than from floppies.  I later changed the Linux boot option in the AlphaBIOS 
setup menu so that the linload.exe program on Disk 0 Partition 1 runs.
I filled in the OS option field with:
 
     boot hdd3:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/hdd3
 
That's it.  I hope that someone else finds this information useful.  I 
currently have everything running except X Windows.  I guess that I will 
have to wait for support of the Matrox Millenium II video card to come out.
 
I first began using Unix when I was 17 years old.  Then, as I went college, 
I used Unix less and less.  Now, I feel as if I have come back to Yoda to 
complete my training.  :-)
 
 
-Ed L



