Sorry, but why would Digital have been doomed ? They had
magneto-resistive heads and all sorts of fancy technology, including the
now popular DLT 
(glorified TK50s :-)

Unfortunatly, what Palmer seems to have done is analyse how the current
Digital would fare and sell off anything that wouldn't survive in
"commodity".
What we, as customers, expected/wanted Palmer to do is to FIX what was
wrong at DEC to allow DEC to compete head to head with the Quantum,
Seagates, HP, COMPAQ etc of the world. And to this date, I am not
convinced the the inherant structural and cultural stuff at DEC has
changed to allow this.

Had Palmer/Digital FIXED what was wrong at DEC and made in into a strong
marketing organisation, and removed that "we are better therefore more
expensive" culture, I am quite confident that Digital could have
succeeded in disks, printers, chips or whatever it wanted. DEC had the
quality , skills and experience to build a wide range of quality
products and quality documentation.  


WHERE THERE IS A WILL, THERE IS A WAY.

Digitals fancy FAB was used only 20-30%. Don't know about you, but if I
had been Palmer and had known about this, I would have told the VP of
that division to start marketing big time to attract customers to that
FAB. Perhaps DEC rested on its laurels after it got the StrongArm and
didn't attempt *ENOUGH* to get more usage out of its fab.

Don't know about you, but if the whole ALPHA thing had been planned to
make money with certain volumes and sales, and this was nowhere near the
targets, I would have started to do marketing big time and perhaps sell
against its own PC business in the commodity arena.  DEC could have gone
"DEC DIRECT" with big time ads and have DEC build PCs in order like
DELL. It would have alleviated DEC's need for channels/retailers.

If you build alarge restaurant and not enough customers come because
meals are too expensive, and you see low end restaurants attracting lost
of customers,
shouldn't you be asking yourself some serious questions ? 

Digital was making money with its revamped "commodity" disk drives and
had JUST begun to get inroads into distributors with commodity SCSI
drives without DEC proprietary stuff on them. But they sold it. Had they
stuck around and pushed hard enough, perhaps Quantum would be the one
having problems today. But I agree that if they hadn't pushed, they
would have been pushed out. It is called competition, it is called
survival of the fittest.

Digital was STARTING to make inroads with commodity PCs, but short term
cash needs forced Palmer to can that plan before their Ad campaign could
get some roots. When you go from 0 to XXX, you cannot expect your
advertising to pay off immediatly. This is a medium effort to build an
image.


LEAD OR BE LEAD. Digital did not have the energy to lead, so it got
sideswiped by the fast running train and is nowhere today even tough it
had the technological assets to lead by a very wide margin.



