From: edlee@chinet.chinet.com (Edward Lee)
Subject: Initial impressions of 21164PC
Date: 1997/11/24
Message-ID: <65bmlo$4k8$1@chinet.chinet.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 292121337
Organization: Chinet
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


I received my 533Mhz 21164PC computer system from Microway on Saturday.
This is the system that they advertise on their web site

     http://www.microway.com

for U.S. $1895.  It comes with 32MB SDRAM, a 24x CDROM drive, a 2GB
EIDE hard drive, Matrox Millenium II video card, Windows NT v4.0,
no monitor.  Look on their web site for exact specifications.  Linux
currently does not run on any PC164SX motherboard, as far as I am
aware, because of the unsupported Cypress CY82C693U chipset(?), as
I read somewhere.  In case someone out there is interesting in
supporting the CY82C693U for Linux, some information on the
CY82C693U can be found at:

     http://www.cypress.com/cypress/prodgate/chip/cy82c693.html

Windows NT v4.0 is bursty in speed on this system and is agonizingly
slow for dial-up web browsing.  One might think that a processor which is
capable of sustained execution well in excess of 50 million integer
instructions per second without breathing hard might be able keep up
with a 115,200 bps modem data stream, but one should never underestimate
the inefficiency of Windows software.  What happens is that bits get
dropped, causing CRC errors, which causes interruptions in data transfers,
which causes pauses and resends.  I put a 16650 serial card in the
machine, but that did not help.  I will be adding more memory to the
machine next month and see if paging is an issue with respect to the
degraded speed in serial port handling.

For non-realtime tasks, the Alpha computer system is generally the fastest
that I have ever used, faster than the Pentium II 300Mhz 128MB RAM computer
system that I use at work.  I was not expecting MSDOS emulation to be
there for the Alpha processor, but it is there for non-protected mode
programs.  The speed of execution for MSDOS programs is bursty on both
the Alpha and Pentium II systems, but the ability to run MSDOS programs
is nice to have anyway.

That is all that I have to say for now.  I hope that someone finds
this information useful.

-Ed L



