Matt Dillon wrote:

>     This is not broken.  This is why you use prototypes and compile -Wall
>     -Wstrict-prototypes ... to allow the compiler to cast the arguments
>     properly for the call.   This is, in fact, one of the primary purposes
>     behind ANSI prototypes and procedure argument specification.
>     To require that exactly the right integer type be passed to a procedure
>     is not good coding practice, it's a pain in the ass and totally
>     non-portable across operating systems.  Even with the mandated library
>     call typing, there are hundreds of other libraries out there unrelated
>     to the ANSI spec that one must deal with.

Matt :

I think we're in a sort of violent agreement, with perhaps slightly
different
viewpoints/philosophies. In your example, you are forcing the compiler
to do the
casting of the parameters, and in mine I used explicit casts. The net
message
remains the same. One way or another, you have to ensure that the
correct type
is passed. If you just trust this stuff to luck, you're writing bad
code. And if
you do it right, you don't have any problems with portability.

The original message of the post I was responding to was that Digital
UNIX
is inherently incompatible with other UNIX implementations. It is, in
fact, not
so. My examples were intended to explicitly demonstrate that Digital
UNIX
does, indeed, use EXACTLY the same data types as do other POSIX
compliant
UNIX variants within these calls. 

Having said that, you're right - variations in C language
implementation,
library implementation, etc., can make it difficult to deal with some
of these portability issues in going between disparate operating
systems.
And using the techniques you described can, in fact, help a lot.

That doesn't diminish the importance of my point about DU being 
unfairly maligned for lack of standards compliance. DU maintains
compliance as good as or better than anything out there. And it
shouldn't be blamed when poorly written code doesn't port well.

Eric D. Osborne

The opinions expressed above are my own, and should not be
interpreted as an official statement by Digital Equipment
Corporation.



