fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu wrote:
>     If  they  were  to  go entirely to IA64,  what  distinguishes  their
>     systems from Dell or a Compac, etc.? 

Well, all Digital really has left that is truly Digital is VMS. Alpha
won't 
ever go to high volumes. It is a niche chip. If DEC were to port VMS to
Intel,
then VMS's clustering capabilities would really shine compared to NT and
UNIX.

On the same "low end Intel" chip, VMS would outperform, be much more
scalable
and be just as affordable as NT. The VMS vs WINDOWS advantage would be
much greater than the OS2 vs WINDOWS so VMS might stand a chance to grab
on to a respectable share of the market. the MAC may not have a big pice
of the pie, but it has a big enough one to be known. VMS isn't anywhere
near that.

>     implement new fab processes.  They  maintain their Alpha chip design
>     team  and are guaranteed access to Intel's best fab process at least
>     until 2005 

Well, considering that DEC was loosing money big time with its
underutilised 
FAB-6, it is evident that the ALPHA chips were being dumped below cost
to the market. Even at that, complete systems with software were not
competing against
WINTEL.

So now, you lower DEC's costs by a certain amount. DOes this
automatically mean that ALPHA will be priced competitively ? Not
necessarily. The cost of ALPHA may still be higher than what the market
is willing to pay, so DEC may have to still subsidize its cost (to a
much lesser extent). In other words, outsourcing production of ALPHA
does not automatically and logically result in an automatic lowering of
production costs below market wishes. It only means that costs are going
to be reduced.

It may lessen the problem; it doesn't necessarily turn Alpha into a
profitable chip.


>     underneath.   The idea of porting VMS to IA64 is a no-go because the
>     VMS engineering effort  is  currently  focussed  on  providing  very
>     high-end capabilities (Galaxy), whereas IA64 would provide a _lower_
>     performance  platform, and at _relatively_ low volume.

With VMS's clustering, it would make VMS outperform other operating
systems running on the same hardware and provide scalability
unattainable on other operating systems, all this running on commodity
hardware.  Very few actually need 
1 million Mhz systems that really need Alpha+Galaxies. But many more
would benefit from combining many lower end INtel boxes into a Galaxy to
provide a serious 
server.


>     one  year!   Having said that, I came away from DECUS  feeling  more
>     confident of VMS' future and Digital's support of VMS than I have at
>     any time in the last five years. 

Analogy: years ago, I hear a Canadian Pacific Railways employee in a
commuter train tell a friend that CP was extremely glad to get rid of
the VIA RAIL passenger train maintenance contract and that they were
looking forward to getting rid of as much railway stuff as possible.

A couple years ago, CP was suddently begging the local government here
to use some of its remaining tracks for commuter trains.

Guess what: those same employees who were so happy to get rid of
anything seen as "legacy" (railway) woke up one day with a company that
had downsized itself almost out of existence, and now, they were
striving to keep what little was left.

I think the same is happening with Digital. VMS is all they got left and
it is barely alive thanks to their past active efforts to kill it
because it was viewed as legacy.

With Alpha future far from certain, I think that Digital *MUST* port VMS
to something else if it wants to insure VMS and its own survival. If
you're not sure 
planet ALPHA will survive because of the ozone hole started years ago,
you should start to populate other planets to ensure the survival of
your species.

And if you do manage to plug the ozone hole and make planet Alpha
succeed, you will have succeeded big time with your own planet healthy
and other planets growing.



