If someone has Linux running on the PC164SX motherboard, then I would
like to know how they got it running, because I would like to maximize the
utilization of the performance potential of my Alpha computer system.
Perhaps the Alpha vendors are simply assuming that they can provide
Linux on all of their Alpha systems because they have been able to do
so in the past.  I believe that Microway also advertises the option of
having Linux preloaded with the system that I have at the same price.  I
chose to get the system with Window NT v4.0, reasoning that I could load
Linux myself on a separate hard drive and have both operating systems,
after a knowledgeable Microway representative informed me that I would
need a separate hard drive if both operating systems were to co-exist.
(An aside: the AlphaBIOS is capable of formatting hard disks with the
FAT format by itself in the setup screen, which I thought was really
convenient.)  I tried using the pc164.img boot image for Linux, but that
just locked up the machine after indicating that it was jumping to PALcode.

>Did you run any benchmarks on 21164PC to compare how it performs, say
>on kernel compilation or X windows performance to a Pentium II ?
>Somebody ought to come out with a Xstone (a la winstone, which fires
>up may be 10 Netscapes in parallel :).

I ran the old eyeball benchmark on screen savers, and the subjective feel
of the computer, aside from the bursty web browsing and MSDOS emulation
that I already mentioned, is snappy with the way graphics get blasted
onto the screen.  Part of the credit for that must go to the Matrox
Millenium II, but if you look at the running graph of processor
utilization while you drag the "thumb" along a vertical scroll bar in
a web browser window, you may see the processor utilization jump up
to ~50% as the window contents smoothly scrolls.  So, part of the credit
for the video speed seems to go to the 21164PC which feeds the video card
at high speed at 1024*768 pixel resolution in true color.  (I would use a
higher resolution, but my current monitor is not up to the task.)  The
system performance would probably be even faster if I had more than
32MB of SDRAM in the system, as documentation included with the
FX!32 software indicates.  (An aside: I generally recommend that people
disable elaborate screen savers, because screen savers will drag down the
performance of a computer system significantly, especially with
Windows NT, which has a tendancy to have its performance drop like a rock
when running multiple applications concurrently.)

-Ed L



