True for normal stuff, of course, but of course Digital could post an 
official statement, or a pointer to such at its website, to the 
newsgroup.

>   Even I get tired of answering the "OpenVMS is dead" questions after
>   a while.  

Imagine how tired all the users are!

>   There will come a date -- sometime in the future -- when
>   there will be no general support available for Microsoft Windows NT,
>   for UNIX, and for all of the other currently-available computer
>   operating systems.  Ask me what that date is, and I can't answer.
>   But I know there will be such a date.  Eventually.

We're all realistic enough to agree to this.  However, there is the 
feeling that VMS will not live out its natural lifespan.  This is bad 
enough, but if it were actually caused by Digital not letting VMS 
realise its full potential, then it is even worse.

>   That said, OpenVMS engineering is still working on and is still
>   developing new releases of OpenVMS, and -- for as long as there is
>   sufficient profit in doing so -- OpenVMS will continue to be enhanced
>   and will continue to be supported.  (RSX is still around and still
>   supported, after all.)  

I'm sure I speak for most of the newsgroup readers when I say that the 
contributions from OpenVMS engineering to this newsgroup (and similar 
ones to comp.lang.fortran from Steve Lionel and others) are extremely 
well appreciated.  Keep up the good work!  I even believe OpenVMS will 
still continue to be developed as long as it turns a profit; one cannot 
expect any development from Digital if it does not.  The only concern 
is that it might stop turning a profit due not to natural reasons but 
due to Digital not selling it enough.

Let me give an example from my own area, astronomy.  This is an area
which relies almost exclusively on public funding and the idealism of
the researchers.  A few years ago, VMS (on VAX) was the platform of
choice.  Then came a migration to UNIX (and usually NOT Digital UNIX)
since it was perceived to be cheaper.  (If one counts all the additional
costs---like additional salaries---then that might not be the case.
However, salaries usually come from one source, computer money from
another.)  Now, of course, VMS is almost as cheap however you cut it
(and probably could be just as cheap) but lack of marketing in this
sector means that the market is essentially lost.  Of course, it doesn't
mean much profit for Digital, but most of the people in
astronomy---especially the ones more interested in computing---go on to
work in other areas.  Lots of places in industry and business, where
there ARE big profits to be made, often give as one reason for going
with unix (which generally means non-Digital UNIX) the fact that it's
easy to hire people who are familiar with it, many of these coming from
academia.  So pushing VMS in the lower-end (workstation as opposed to
server, not necessarily referring to speed) would give it a bigger
presence in academia which, though perhaps not profitable in itself,
would `trickle up' to other areas.  Even outside of academia, as someone
pointed out here recently, people don't buy 8400s to WRITE and DEVELOP
applications.  One of the strengths of VMS has always been that it is
the same across the whole range of machines.  And in many areas,
especially the highly-profitable ones, applications sell systems.  VMS
profits go to Digital.  NT profits go, at least to a large extent, to
Micro$oft\footnote{I thought the dollar sign in logical names was
reserved to Digital---oops, Micro$oft is not a logical name:}, and even
Digital UNIX leaves the danger that it is easier to switch to another
vendor, so those customers probably aren't as a long-term source of
profits as VMS customers. 

> And we -- OpenVMS engineering -- are hiring.

Are you hiring in Europe?


