>Jerome Fine  <jhfine@idirect.com> wrote:
>>The Zip drive cartridges are exactly 3
>>partitions in size which makes for a convient
>>media of distribution (at 100 MBytes or
>>65,536 * 3 = 196,608 blocks).
>
>FYI, at least *some* controllers (CDU-720/M in particular) deduct one block
>from this size for use as a one-block RCT (the MSCP spec requires this first
>RCT block even if the device doesn't use RA8x-style bad block replacement,
>even though with floppies, DEC's own controllers don't bother with this rule).

And it's not clear to me if removable ZIP media would be interchangable
between CMD host adapters, Emulex SCSI host adapters, Dilog host adapters,
etc.  At least some SCSI host adapters (i.e. Webster/Sigma SCSI host
adapters with Wombat) write partition table information at the start
of the media so this information doesn't have to be stored in nonvolatile
memory on the controller.

Removable-SCSI-media interchangability is, indeed, a difficult
problem.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



