Full Text : 
ZEUS TECHNOLOGY RELEASES NEXT GENERATION OF INTERNET WEB SERVER
SOFTWARE 

On November 21st 1997, Zeus Technology Limited of Cambridge, England,
announces the release of a major new version of its web server
software, Zeus Web Server v3.  The product is a scaleable web
application server suitable for services which require bullet-proof
reliablity and exceptionally high performance.  Initial market areas
include ISP hosting, secure e-Commerce, high volume web sites and
Intranet application servers.      

The product has the most comprehensive virtual server support of any
web server, there can be an unlimited number of virtual servers with
groupable configuration, real-time statistics, bandwidth throttling,
extensive logging and a web centric delegatable management
interface. This makes the Zeus Web Server v3.0 the ideal choice for
web-hosting companies and Internet service providers.  

Application development is available in ISAPI, Java-Servlets, secure
CGI and ZDAC (Zeus Distributed Authentication and Content API)
providing load-balancing and fault-tolerance to
applications. Distributed server-side Java gives high-performance
database connectivity.    

Full Microsoft Frontpage and Netscape publishing support is included
in the package providing enterprise-wide site management and content
publishing support.  The software has the strongest encryption
commercially available worldwide using the latest version of SSL,
version 3.  Apache htaccess support allows easy migration of existing
Apache websites to the Zeus Server.   

On top of all these features, Zeus Technology has stayed true to its
tradition of offering highly reliable and efficient software
meaning that the hardware requirements are still very low.  Over the
last three years the web has changed beyond all recognition. These
changes have happened because web developers have pushed the
technology to its limits.  Zeus Web Server v3 pushes back the
limits again, giving web developers the tools they need to produce
tomorrow's websites today.

Supported platforms : Digital Unix 4.0, IRIX 6.3, HP-UX 11, Solaris
Sparc 2.5.  Most major Unix platforms appearing soon, Windows NT first
quarter 98. 

