   The market window for the 21164PC has probably long closed.  It
   just isn't good enough be have the same or somewhat higher
   performance than x86;  MIPS and PowerPC tried this and failed
   miserably in the NT arena.  I am not sure a simple shrink will
   do enough to save the 21164PC unless the L1 caches are doubled
   and the clock rate upped significantly.

   I think DEC would best be served by shrinking 21164 (21164B ?) and
   adding the bus tweaks from 21164PC as a low cost place holder until
   a 21264PC design can be readied.   As ultra high performance is Alpha's
   only ace card you could even argue that all this 21x64PC stuff is counter-
   productive by diverting engineering effort from the high end cores
   and possibly reducing high end volumes.  It seems that when the low
   end parts finally make it into production the performance is actually
   embarassing (like the 21066) and detracts from Alpha's mystique.

> 
> > Didn't we hear numbers like 14.x specint95 for 533MHz 21164PC ?
> 
> 14/17 were the estimates a year ago. 12.6/16.1 are the current
> (and one would imagine more accurate) estimates.
> 
> Maybe the numbers will be better with the motherboards Mitsubishi
> is currently making based on VLSI's Polaris chipset. The first
> version of Polaris supports an 83MHz bus, as opposed to the
> DEC chipset's 66MHz bus. The next version of Polaris will support
> a 100MHz bus.
> 
> But even without the faster buses, it looks like the 21164PC will
> give hugely better fp and multimedia performance than the PII-300.

   The FP application with maximum relevance to PC users is 3D
   graphics and here more and more of the rendering pipeline stages
   are being moved into the 3D chipset as being more cost effective.

   Intel might actually be feeling the effect of cheap and effective 3D
   cards in the slower than expected accceptance of Pentium II into the
   mainstream.  Maybe Intel will have to bring back its "red circle with
   the slash through it" campaign to finally kill classic Pentium and
   herd the sheep to PII   ;-)

> 
