   Well, the 21164PC isn't in production yet AFAIK;  If and when it does
   it will come up against >300 MHz Deschutes version of Pentium II.  The
   SPECfp95 performance is great (whatever it turns out to be in actual
   products) but is almost irrelevent for the audience DEC is targeting the
   21164PC to.  And if the 211164PC doesn't have a noteworthy advantage in
   SPECint95 over Intel's latest then it will be the kiss of death.

   Take the lesson of the "low cost" Alpha 21066; this was a 21064 with cut
   down bus (memory controller + PCI bus).  The fastest 21066 numbers I have
   seen are for a 233 MHz 21066A which wheezed out 97 SPECint92, and 112
   SPECfp92.

   For comparison purposes, a 133 MHz Pentium yields 174.2 SPECint92 and
   109.6 SPECfp92 and a 150 MHz Pentium Pro turns out 245 SPECint92 and
   220 SPECfp92.

   Nothing sadder than to see a posting by someone who bought an "Alpha
   board" which turns out to be 21066 based and wonders why the damn thing
   was slower than a garage sale Pentium  :-(   It's a self inflicted wound
   by someone who didn't do any research but it still makes DEC and Alpha
   look bad.  I predict a similar future for 21164PC based systems.  There
   is no place for Alpha's low cost B-team players.

   BTW,

   If DEC wants variety in its design challenges why not see how much
   absolute performance it could get from a "money is no object"* Alpha CPU
   that meets the Intel power specs for mobile processor applications (8 Watts
   average or whatever).  Since RISC's advantage over CISC seems maximal for
   restricted implementations (like embedded control processors) maybe there
   will be a market in NT laptops for performance hungry users with money to
   burn for a low power, high performance Alpha with a big advantage over x86
   (look how poorly x86 compares to PowerPC in the 5-10 Watt CPU range).

   * within reason of course.  But remember, Intel gets its cushiest margins
     on those high priced, cheap to build, P55C MMX Pentiums for laptops.
> 
>    -doug

