David Evans <dfevans@bbcr.uwaterloo.ca> writes:
>   I want to have an RX50 and an RX33 in my uVax so I'm going to install a
> second RQDX3.  Is it as simple as setting a correct CSR, installing another
> distribution board above the one I already have, and cabling everything up?  If
> the RX33 is the only drive on the second RQDX3 do I still have to attach it to
> the floppy connector at the top of the distribution board?  Will "most" 5.23"
> 1.2MB floppy drives work as an RX33?  I have a genuine one but I'd like to use
> a beige drive to match the BA123's front.  :)

  Why do you want both the RX50 and the RX33? The RX33 is quite happy reading
and writing RX50 media. You could stack a pair of RX33's if you wanted to do
direct floppy-to-floppy copies (the only other advantage I can see with the
RX50).

  Using a second M9058 signal distribution board is unsupported. I don't know
of anyone that's done it, but it might work.

  "Real" RX33's are Teac FD-55GFV's, as I recall. Back in the old days any
Teac 1.2MB PC drive could be jumpered as an RX33, but since the Teac has made
all sorts of cost-cutting steps on their 5.25" drives, and the most recent
ones I've seen don't have any jumpers at all. However, the faceplates should
still be interchangeable.

  For *lots* of info on substitutes for DEC's RX- and RD-series drives, get
the file ftp://ftp.spc.edu/third-party-disks.txt.

