I'm pretty sure the reason was timing:  Alpha wasn't ready, and Apple
could do a lot of development work on production RS/6000 workstations
while the PowerPC chips were being prepared.  I think that waiting for
Alpha would have been at least a six-month and possibly a two-year
penalty, compared to going with PowerPC. 

(If I recall correctly, the PowerPC architecture was an extension of 
the RS/6000 specification, adding single-precision floating point, or 
some such modest group of instructions.) 

