Digital has always refused to go "low cost high volume" and protected
themselves with the "we are high quality therefore worth more" flag.

It is sad that this did not work , and even sadder that "low quality
high volume" (aka: Microsoft and Intel) work so well (tons of profits).

Digital now has proof that low volume is not sustainable. It has had to
sell off its alpha production lines because they were loosing too much
money. Perhaps if Palmer had focused (a word he likes to use, right ?)
on converting itself from high-cost-low-volume to low-cost-high-volume
instead of concentrating on musical chairs reorganisations and layoffs,
Digital might have made Alpha popular enough to be sustainable and kept
DEC's fabs busy.

When PALMER got in, he should have slashed C compiler prices from $3000
down to $ 300 before even firing anyone.

The sad fact is that I have no confidence that Digital has learned its
lesson and thus no confidence that DEC will more to high volume low cost
operations, it is far easier to find a market niche and be comfortable
in it and make customers captive and pay more. But I do not think that
it is a viable option in the computer industry, unless you end up making
custom products for large projects (NASA, military etc).



