In article <3473FFD0.2C13BAF7@passau.netsurf.de>,
"Christian J. Bauer" <christian.bauer@passau.netsurf.de> wrote:
> Jonathan Boswell wrote:
> 
> > Yup.  You missed the fact that the DSSI node ID is separate and distinct
> > from the unit number.  The former can take on only values from 0 to 7,
> > and must be unique on a single DSSI bus.  The unit number, on the other
> > hand, is used to make the device name in VMS unique in a multiple-DSSI
> > bus configuration, where far more than 7 devices are possible.
> 
> Hmmm, but wouldnt  my DIA0: then would be a DIB0: ?

All recent versions of VMS call all DSSI disks DIAx:.
The traditional controller letter naming would restrict cluster configuration,
as this would force you to connect the A bus of one system to the A bus of
another, the B bus of one system to the B bus of another, etc.  Otherwise the
same device would show up twice (once as DIA0: and DIB0: for example if you
connected bus A on one system to B on another)  This restriction would forbid
the "triangle" configuration, with 3 nodes with 2 busses each connected in a
triangle, unless you added "extra" adapters to some systems to get the
controller letter on other busses "right".

Ancient versions of VMS *did* name dssi devices DIBx:, DICx: etc. This was
changed to prevent the unnecessary restrictions mentioned.  This same problem
bit DEC again recently (even harder) when they developed SCSI clustering.

-Mike



