Anil Thomas Maliyekkel wrote:
> 
> 
> Because the interfaces necessary to take advantage of 64 bit address
> spaces and 64 bit file systems involve using differ data types
> for the parameters.  Most UNIX applications have been written to use
> 32 bit paramaters for file system operations and have been written
> to take advantage of 32 address spaces.  32 bit UNIX 95 compliant
> OSes share the identical interfaces which makes portibility between
> these OSes much easier.  Since Digital decided to eschew 32 bit
> compatibility in Digital Unix, it made it harder for companies
> to port applications over.  Also paging characteristics for 64 bit
> OSes makes performance and memory consumption optimization more
> difficult for programs that were designed to run optimally on
> 32 bit OSes.

This kind of thing really bugs me, because it is just blatantly WRONG.
Digital UNIX on ALPHA is pretty much always fully source compatible with
Solaris and HP/UX, assuming PROPERLY (e.g., standards compliant code).

The biggest portability problem is sloppy coding. If you write
code to the proper ANSI standards, at a source level it is transparent
(or nearly so) when moving between Digital UNIX and Sun/HP/whatever
systems. I've done a LOT of this, and in general, once I've ported
the code it will compile without a hitch on other vendor systems.

Case in point - the POSIX.1 and  XPG standards for file I/O primitives
use the defined type t_offset to describe the file position pointer
in lseek, and size_t for read, write, etc. These object types - 
t_offset, size_t, etc., are abstractions that are defined in the system
header file sys/types.h

A good ANSI compiler (we have a good one :-) ) will complain if you
use the wrong parameter types on the calls (and well it should). So,
if you have code like this

	int	f;		/* file descriptor */
	int	ret_code;	/* for return code checking */
	char	buffer[BUFSIZE];
	/* put in code to open file here */
	ret_code = read(f, buffer, (size_t) sizeof(buffer));


this will work on ANYTHING that is standards compliant. The problem
is, people do dumb stuff like this :
	int	f;
	int	ret_code, size;
	char	buffer[BUFSIZE];
	/* put in code to open file here */
	ret_code = read(f, buffer, size);

THIS IS BROKEN, because the "size" parameter passed to the read 
call is of the wrong data type. This will, by blind luck, work on most
32 bit machines. It will break on DU, BUT NOT BECAUSE DU IS 
NON-STANDARD. It will break because it is crappy code. Fixing it will
make it work on EVERYTHING, and eliminate a potential maintenence 
headache from the source pool forever.

Even at run-time, Solaris and HP/UX will allow you to get away with
really poor coding. Dereferencing of null pointers, for instance,
is a BAD THING, and it happens in a lot of crufty code. Sometimes
it gets by, depending on architecture. The TLI allocation calls on 
at least some versions of Solaris do a very poor job of checking
the integrity of passed arguments. When broken (by the definition 
of the STANDARDS) code is moved from those machines to DU, developers
are often quick to blame DU because the code doesn't work - when it is
more likely bugs in the original source. 

I can produce examples to demonstrate this kind of thing. I would be
delighted to see one where code written to standards, which does not
INTENTIONALLY exploit object size differneces or byte order, doesn't 
port cleanly. Specifically, I would challenge anyone to show me any
significant set of DU library or system calls that don't conform to
the appropriate UNIX standards. About the only time you get into any
trouble at all is when you make assumptions about object sizes - and
that is a violation of the C language standard.

At the risk of being inflamatory, I can cope with legitimate criticism
as well as anyone. But I have an issue with highly opinionated,
negative postings that have no basis in fact.

Eric D. Osborne

The opinions expressed herein are my own, and should not be construed
to represent any official statement by Digital Equipment Corporation.



