:>THIS IS BROKEN, because the "size" parameter passed to the read 
:>call is of the wrong data type. This will, by blind luck, work on most

    This is not broken.  This is why you use prototypes and compile -Wall
    -Wstrict-prototypes ... to allow the compiler to cast the arguments
    properly for the call.   This is, in fact, one of the primary purposes
    behind ANSI prototypes and procedure argument specification.
    To require that exactly the right integer type be passed to a procedure 
    is not good coding practice, it's a pain in the ass and totally 
    non-portable across operating systems.  Even with the mandated library
    call typing, there are hundreds of other libraries out there unrelated
    to the ANSI spec that one must deal with.

    Anybody who is not compiling their code prototype-clean is writing 
    seriously broken code.   In many cases it's just a matter of making
    the proper #include's and fixing all the warnings the compiler throws
    with -Wall -Wstrict-prototypes.  If you do that, you are as close to safe
    as it's possible to write something like:

	int x = sizeof(buf);
	int n;
	char buf[256];
	..
	n = read(fd, buf, x);

	or

	n = read(fd, buf, sizeof(buf));

    and have it be perfectly valid code.  If you want an example of the 
    clean prototype methodology, take a look at the Diablo source tree at:

	http://www.backplane.com/diablo/

:>Eric D. Osborne

						-Matt




