In article <gurman-1811970759330001@goodgulf.nascom.nasa.gov>, 
    				gurman@ari.net (Joseph B. Gurman) writes:
[...]
>     Are there plans for porting OVMS to the IA-64 architecture? The
> statement that:
> 
>> DIGITAL
>> will continue manufacturing Alpha systems as long as there is a customer
>> demand for them
> 
> is not one to inspire boundless confidence or commitment in the breasts of
> users with substantial OpenVMS investments, since it could be read as,
> "Once the NT business has switched to Merced and Digital UNIX can be
> migrated in that direction, we'll stop any further development of Alphas
> that keep up with IA-64 speed improvements" [not a real quote, just my
> speculation], because there won't be sufficient demand to warrant spending
> on the necessary R&D. Manufacturing, yes, continued development, no. That,
> of course, would leave OpenVMS with no future.

        Jesse Lipcom's statements at  DECUS  would indicate, first, that
    Digital  fully  expects Alpha to maintain a significant,  i.e.,  2:1
    speed advantage over Merced for the foreseeable future.  That is, at
    the time Merced appears (1999 on a 0.18 micron process), Alpha  will
    also  be released on the 0.18 micron process and have at least a 2:1
    performance advantage over Merced.  Digital  _expects_ to keep Alpha
    systems the highest performing systems in all OSes (VMS, unix & NT).
    If  they  were  to  go entirely to IA64,  what  distinguishes  their
    systems from Dell or a Compac, etc.?  Second, the  deal  with  Intel
    _enhances_  the  viability and lifetime of Alpha since Digital is no
    longer saddled with the research and capital investiment required to
    implement new fab processes.  They  maintain their Alpha chip design
    team  and are guaranteed access to Intel's best fab process at least
    until 2005 (he didn't _say_ the agreement  says  2005,  he  said  he
    "wouldn't  confirm  or  deny"  reports  in  the  press  that the fab
    agreement extends till 2005...and  could  be extended past that...).
    Third,  the  agreement  speeds  up  the  roadmap  for  future  Alpha
    enhancements (I think Steve H.  said as much in his  post).   Forth,
    the  viability  of  Alpha  assures  the viability of VMS (so long as
    there are VMS and  Alpha  customers),  because  VMS depends on Alpha
    underneath.   The idea of porting VMS to IA64 is a no-go because the
    VMS engineering effort  is  currently  focussed  on  providing  very
    high-end capabilities (Galaxy), whereas IA64 would provide a _lower_
    performance  platform, and at _relatively_ low volume.  There are no
    spare engineering resources  to  put  into  such  a marginal effort.
    [And  it  would make a lot more sense to many readers of these  news
    groups to release VMS on low-end Alphas, e.g.,  the  21164PC,  which
    should be as cost effective as an Intel platform...]

>     Having said that, I would hasten to point out that Digital has done
> very well indeed by VAX owners over the past ~5 years of Alpha production,
> and has continued to improve VAX price/performance, even though those
> machines could easily be considered "legacy" systems. I think it's
> important that Digital actually address its OVMS customers directly and
> tell them what the VMS business model is for the next five years, and the
> expectations for beyond that.

        I don't think they have  five  years  worth of vision, let alone
    one  year!   Having said that, I came away from DECUS  feeling  more
    confident of VMS' future and Digital's support of VMS than I have at
    any time in the last five years.  It seems that VMS is no  longer  a
    dirty  word  within Digital (although the sales force probably don't
    know it exists), there is  _significant_ development going into VMS,
    and  Bruce  Claflin seems to understand what stupidity  Digital  has
    displayed with respect to (not) marketing VMS in the recent (and not
    so recent) past.  We'll just have to wait and  see  whether  Claflin
    actually makes a _difference_ or not...

        -Ken
