In article <34736D9D.71CD@videotron.ca>,
jf mezei  <"[non-spam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca> wrote:
>Digital has always refused to go "low cost high volume" and protected
>themselves with the "we are high quality therefore worth more" flag.

	Not true.  DEC was founded on the premise of the "low cost,
high volume" market.  The story goes that the early DEC computers were
called PDPs, Programmable Data Processors, because the market forecast
for computers was so small (in terms of volume) that DEC wouldn't have
been able to secure financing had they announced that their product
line was focusing on "computers".  In the 60's and early 70's, DEC's
primary philosophy was to produce inexpensive systems, and sell more
of them than any competitor.

	Once DEC had established its reputation, and produced some
pretty fine products (thanks to both DEC employee's and DEC's
customers), DEC switched to a medium-cost high-volume tack: constant
price, increasing performance.  DEC focused on VAX/VMS their single
mid-to-upper computer line, eliminating much of the internal product
competition that had stimulated innovation in the company (DEC used to
sell multiple competing computers systems in its small- and mid-level
markets) to cement consumer awareness of the VAX/VMS brands.

	Having switched to the "constant cost" market approach, DEC
allowed other companies to get ahead of them on the actual market
trend: lower cost, increasing performance.  This culminated in DEC's
inability to compete in the PC marketplace due, in my view, to two
primary blunders: 1) DEC's unwillingness to capitalize on the existing
rich PDP-8 and PDP-11 software bases by agressively introducing cheap
PCs based upon them (despite a valiant effort by Heathkit) (in the
short term, it would have gutted DEC's mid-level price structure), and
2) DEC's failure to recognize the nature of the emerging IBM-basedPC
market by introducing "IBM-compatibles" that weren't.

	In further strategic mis-moves, DEC lost dominance of the
workstation market to competitors such as Sun.  DEC focused on
corporate stategies that isolated them from external innovation: for
example, DEC's DECnet and VMS policies made it difficumat to
assimilate VMS systems into the ARPAnet and Internet, unlike DEC's
earlier PDP-10, -11, and -20 systems, which had served as the primary
development platforms in the Internet's formative periods.

	By the mid-80s DEC had lost entry to these emergent
high-volume markets.  However, DEC now had a substantial VAX/VMS
customer base that was willing to pay premium prices for reliable
operation.  Thus, DEC could operate for a few more years in a "high
cost, low volume" mode.

					Craig Milo Rogers



