You don't have to segment the UNIX market very carefully at all to reach
the conclusion that Digital UNIX is in (IMHO) a pretty healthy state.
Please permit me to refer you to IDC's recent report "UNIX Systems
Market Review and Forecast, 1996-2001" (IDC #14468, October 1997).

IDC shows overall Digital UNIX revenue and market share as *growing* not
shrinking. IDC estimates Digital UNIX revenues at $1.554B in CY1996, up
18.2% over CY1995. The overall UNIX market grew 11.3% in that time, so
Digital UNIX grew faster than the market, and increased market share.
Digital is the #5 UNIX vendor. ( IDC #14468, Table 6, P21)

In "Entry" UNIX servers (servers selling for $10K to $100K) IDC also
shows Digital UNIX increasing market share. Digital is the #4 vendor
here, with CY1996 revenues of $688M. Growth was 38.2% from '95-'96,
which, again, is faster than the 21.6% growth in the Entry server
segment. Digital UNIX revenues and market share are increasing. (IDC
#14468, Table 11, p31)

In "Midrange" UNIX servers (servers selling for $100K to $1M) IDC shows
Digital UNIX CY1996 revenue more than doubling: up 104.1% to $427M. This
growth is nearly four times the 26.7% growth of this segment. (IDC
#14468, Table 13, p32)

The growth in revenue and share continues in 1997. In our Q1 earnings
anouncement, Digital noted that UNIX AlphaServer sales were up 30%
(http://www.digital.com/info/finance/q198earn.html). IDC forecasts that,
on the average, the UNIX server market will grow by 8.9%  (IDC #14468,
Table 29, p47) Digital UNIX AlphaServers are growing three times faster
than the market.

Increasing revenue. Increasing market share. I suspect that at least
some analysts would describe that as healthy.





