In article <64akhi$9v4@usenet.pa.dec.com>, hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam wrote:

[snip]
> 
>   DIGITAL will maintain the design and engineering team that created
>   Alpha and the current Alpha microprocessor designs, and the designers
>   and engineers will continue working on future Alpha microprocessors.
>   (More on this below...)  And -- per Robert Palmer's comments -- DIGITAL
>   will continue manufacturing Alpha systems as long as there is a customer
>   demand for them.
> 
[snip]

    Mr. Hoffman -

    Thanks for an informed view. Most people simply do not realize how
incredibly expensive new fabs are, and how long in advance they must be
planned.

    What you fail to address, however, is the implication in Mr. Palmer's
public statements that the future for NT and Digital UNIX eventually lies
with IA-64's, not Alphas, and, at least by omission, that OpenVMS isn't on
that bus.

    Are there plans for porting OVMS to the IA-64 architecture? The
statement that:

> DIGITAL
> will continue manufacturing Alpha systems as long as there is a customer
> demand for them

is not one to inspire boundless confidence or commitment in the breasts of
users with substantial OpenVMS investments, since it could be read as,
"Once the NT business has switched to Merced and Digital UNIX can be
migrated in that direction, we'll stop any further development of Alphas
that keep up with IA-64 speed improvements" [not a real quote, just my
speculation], because there won't be sufficient demand to warrant spending
on the necessary R&D. Manufacturing, yes, continued development, no. That,
of course, would leave OpenVMS with no future.

    Having said that, I would hasten to point out that Digital has done
very well indeed by VAX owners over the past ~5 years of Alpha production,
and has continued to improve VAX price/performance, even though those
machines could easily be considered "legacy" systems. I think it's
important that Digital actually address its OVMS customers directly and
tell them what the VMS business model is for the next five years, and the
expectations for beyond that.

    IMHO.

                  Joe Gurman

