On all my systems, I have always used a rotational tape method.

I do a full image backup on Monday and incrementals Tuesday to Friday.
Users were informed that weekends were not backed up except under special
request. This set of tapes for a period of five days got put away, and
week two started, same process. Then week three, then week four. Week 5
went back to Week ones tapes. Once a month, I did a full image backup and
that is what I sent off-site for storage.

The DLT has very excellent reuse capability, exceeding everything in the
business, and this set of tpes used for backups is probably good for
twenty years of reuse, but let's be conservative, 10 years is good. Your
accountants will tell you how long you have to retain your full backups
off-site, but some require by law, 7 years of records or the like.

I ran all this with a DCL command procedure I wrote.

Today, you could take a look at Networker, it manages all this for you and
has the added advantage that Networker clients are available for
practically everything from UNIX to PC's all feeding into the same
Networker Server.

Your DIGITAL rep. would be more than happy to consult with you on this
issue if you like.

rtt

