Gerald (Jerry) R. Leslie  jerry.leslie@aspentech.com  Aspen Technology,
Inc. wrote (in part):

:Another EDT-like editor is SEDT, available from this site:
:
:  http://www.ultranet.com/~anker/sedt/sedt_main.htm
:  Sedt editor home page

I'm an old DEC/VMS fart that has used SEDT extensively both while at DEC
and after leaving DEC... and it's the ONLY way I'm surviving in the land
of Unix editors (shudder). If you're an old hand at EDT then getting SEDT
running is worth your efforts. The main 'good' features of SEDT are:

- It's FREE!!
- Multi-buffer support
- Multiple executions of any key function
- Learned key sequences
- Column mode cuts/pastes
- "Find the matching close character" - Lets you find the closing paren,
curly brace or square bracket when doing edits on source code (if you are
doing C or Perl programming, this is a godsend).

Spend some time in the docs understanding these and other powerful
features- it will be worth it!

Anker Berg-Sonne deserves a lot of credit and thanks... it was an
after-hours project for him while he was at DEC and it was very
successful.

Here are some tips I compiled on SEDT:

1) You can choose either 'native' sedt key layout or emulate DEC EDT
editor. I chose the EDT. Details are in the docs.

2) Using it from a terminal emulator on a PC to a remote system (Unix
in my case) works fine, but if you do some of the 'trick' stuff (like
learned key sequences) then you need to do some funny keystrokes, e.g.

SEDT function            On DEC VT                            On PC

Begin Learn Key Sequence  <PF1><F17>  <Num Lock><Shift F7>

Goto buffer 1  <F18>  <Num Lock><Shift F8>

As you can see, the higher numbered 'Function' keys are emulated by doing
a shift- F8 shifted is F18, etc.

3) The PC data entry keypad has one less key than a DEC LKxxx keypad, so
the <Ctrl KP+> key ends up being the 'Delete Word under the cursor' key.

4)I never cared for the 'Windows' version of SEDT (quirky for me), but
the DOS version seemed to do OK under windows (i.e. I could use the same
keystrokes EDT keystrokes that I was used to). No long filename support
in either version, though.

5) For those of you in Unix land, I have had success getting the DEC OSF
(well, called Digital Unix this week) version running as well as the Linux
version. You must make sure that the SEDT directory is in your path for
things to work.

6) If you want to use the console tube itself on a PC running some flavor
of Unix then things get really strange. You're better off accessing the
Unix PC via telnet and a terminal emulator. If you must use SEDT direct
from the Unix PC console you will need to hack a keymap file (Linux in my
case) and then load it when you log in via the PC keyboard. I can send
you a Linux keymap file if you email to the address at the bottom of the
page.

7) My PC terminal emulator/telnet client of choice is called 'CRT' and it
is at  http://www.vandyke.com/ . I set it up to act like a VT220 and set
the 'backspace key as delete' and SEDT seemes to work very nicely. If all
else fails, you should at least be able to use the PC <F8> as 'quit' and
<F10> as exit until you get it to work.

8) The VMS version of SEDT should work fine... I used it for years at
customer sites with no problems.

9) To 'spawn out' to a subprocess, do a 'Gold Return' (on PC keyboard
<NumLock><Return>). When done, log out of the process and you're back in
SEDT. Use with caution: You might spawn out and forget and go back and
start editing the same file again. If you logout and go back into
SEDT and do a 'save' then you just overwrote your new stuff with the old
(been there, done that). Do a 'quit' instead of a 'save'.

10) Take a few minutes to read though the docs and help text. Will save
you lots of time later.

11) When doing file operations like a 'get' or a 'save', when sedt
prompts for 'File:' along the screen bottom, you can use the up or down
arrow to scroll though previous stuff you have entered.

Good luck!

Lee Roth
roth@tpusa.com

