jf mezei wrote:
> 
> Question to Mr Shannon:
> 
> Did you get a feeeling that the shareholders were happy with the
> direction DIgital was heading ?
> 
> Did Palmer get support for the DEC-INTEL "thing"  ?

Indeed he did. Despite rumours that neither Palmer nor Grove were
delighted with the settlement, Palmer touted the agreement as an
opportunity for the firm to disencumber itself from the hideous
cost of being a merchant microprocessor vendor.

This in itself sounded strange coming from the lips of a "chip guy," but
the handwriting was on the wall for Fab-6. Running at only 20 to 30
percent capacity even with StrongARM, the fab was destined to be a
long-term headache. And, as Palmer pointed out, analysts were
justifiably concerned about the enormous costs associated with a Fab-7
plant. Shortly after Y2K, DEC would have found it necessary to build yet
another fab to support processes smaller than 0.18 micron. This followon
plant would have cost at least three times what Fab-6 cost.

Incidentally, Palmer also alluded to another Alpha architecture partner.
I suspect we'll be hearing something out of AMD Real Soon Now.

> 
> Are shareholders happy with DEC's financials and hence do not see a need
> for a board change of a Digital strategic direction/plan ????

Speaking as a shareholder myself, I am not delighted that $2K of HP or
Sun stock purchased in 1992 would be worth over $10K today while $2K of
DEC stock purchased in 1992 would be worth about $2K today.

Stockholders were pleased that DEC's stock value has doubled since last
April, but not sufficiently pleased to accept the status quo. One of the
stockholder initiatives--setting the terms of the BoD so that all
appointments expire at the same time--reflected the ongoing concern.

I was surprised that Digital's shareholder proxy did not include any new
BoD nominees; I expect things will be different next year.

I am in the midst of a write-up on the annual meeting, my subscribers
should see same within the next few days.

Regards,
Terry Shannon
