Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec.micro


You have an educational problem on your hands, probably.

The users are most likely turning off the terminals without logging out of
the DECserver completely. This basically leaves a floating line hanging on
to the port, and given that your cabling is over the 50 foot guarenteed
for 9600 Baud, the floating line gets some garbage on it, the DECserver
thinks it saw and XOFF and voila, Hung port.

Educate your users to ensure that they log out completely. If need be, get
one of those nice Brother Lmainated Label Printers and do the following.
Print a custom label for each terminal, it should have the reminder, Make
Sure you Log out completely, and should also have the DECserver LAT and
Port number, as well as the number to call for logout. You could
optionally write a DCL script that would log out the port in question,
create a special captive account with enough privilege to run the DCL
script, ask for LAT and Port and then log out the problem port, then log
out the user. Placing the burden of correcting the problem on the users
will enable them to appreciate the problem of failing to log out
completely.

rtt

