Multiple live sessions over a standard serial dial up line is made through a
Digital multiplexing protocol called TDSMP.  TDSMP is supported by the VT420
terminal and most terminal servers.  Most terminal servers also support a
break function that allows switching between multiple sessions but these are
not live displayable sessions - merely switching back and forth.

From home, your friend is likely to require a PC and an emulator product (I
don't know how to get multiple live sessions with a real terminal when
dialing up).  They can telnet in and bypass all TDSMP concerns if their host
is on the network (running IP) and they have access to a PPP or SLIP dialup
account.

If the host is not on the network, they will need to dial into a terminal
server (though it is possible to dial directly into a modem attached to a
host).  To get multiple sessions they will need to purchase a product called
KEAlink MSU and KEA! 420 from Attachmate.  I work for Attachmate and can
heartily recommend these products not to mention that we are the only
company that has licensed the protocol from Digital.  However, we are
archiving the KEAlink MSU product from market due to the decline of serial
access to hosts so if your friend needs it, she should contact Attachmate
very soon (http://www.attachmate.com/osg).

The PC solution would likely make the most sense since there are a number of
excellent data to voice packages to provide vision impaired employees with
access to all applications running on the system.

Hope this helps,
Paulo Chow
Attachmate Open Systems Group

