Here it is a little more elegant, without using "_asm" statements.
This version appeared in Bugtraq.

char x [5] = { 0xf0, 0x0f, 0xc7, 0xc8 };
main ()
{
        void (*f)() = x;
        f();
}

As I already said in a previous posting, I'm not too much
concerned about that bug. No compiler will ever generate
these 4 bytes in its code section - unless the compiler
has some "strange" bug, or you deliberately write down that
code, as shown above.

Of course, there will be viruses, trojan horses, evil Active X
applets that will try to stop user's computers with this
Pentium bug. But this attack is rather harmless compared to
the other things that even Word macro viruses can do.

