Jim Koehler wrote:
> 
> Has anyone gotten the ATI MACH64 GX ISA card (DEC #PB2GA-FB) to work in the
> AXP 150 (aka Jensen) under OpenVMS? It works fine under Windows NT 4.0 and VMS
> just doesn't see it at all (V6.2 and V7.1). Even installing the drivers that
> come on diskette doesn't do the trick for VMS.
> 
> Any ideas? If I can't get this card to work, I guess I'll try to find a Compaq
> QVISION (which reports say it does work).
> 
> Jim Koehler
> Email: JDK at CAMPUS dot COM (read that aloud)
> (delete the -nospam- from e-mail address in the headers)


  Well, I briefly considered doing the Mach64 work on the Jensen,
  since I already had the Mach32 working...  there were two problems:

  1) The console did not recognize it.  That is, something in the
     BIOS emulation on the Jensen did not work right, and I could
     not even get the console to come up.  The console is in a
     maintenance mode, so it is not possible to get new development
     to happen for an unsupported option.

  2) The Mach64, unlike say, the Mach32, has a 8mb (or is it 4mb?
     I don't remember offhand) address window when you are programming
     it using the memory mapped registers (they are at the end of the
     address window).  The Jensen is pretty primitive in its IO and
     memory decoding, so the address window would need to be placed at
     PA 8mb for 8mb (on the ISA Mach64 the window needs to be in the
     base 16mb).

     The gotcha is that you can't do DMA to any *real* memory in the
     same address range as the EISA memory.  It's random if memory,
     or the IO device will respond... and VMS does not have a way to
     block out "some" memory as being usable *except* as the source
     or destination of a DMA operation.  So... the physical address
     range where the address window is located would need to have its
     memory set to bad pages.

     The older cards worked because I put the 1mb frame buffer address
     space at PA 1mb, which is in the PA range that the console is
     loaded, and which VMS does not use.  Since the console never
     does DMA to itself, there is never a conflict with the graphics
     device.

     More modern Alpha systems provide adequate "PC Hole" registers,
     and Direct Map Windows that allow us to mask the first 8mb of
     ISA/EISA space out, and allow these older devices to work.

     The NT drivers are either running: VGA mode, IO register mode,
     or are able to take advantage of the NT ability to *not* do DMA
     transfers to legacy address spaces.

