Organization: CEC Karlsruhe
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <EJAAHp.10B@cwi.nl> pmontgom@cwi.nl (Peter L. Montgomery) writes:
>	Using the cc and f90 compilers now present on Digital UNIX
>systems, how does one access UMULH (upper half of unsigned integer 
>multiply)? 

Have a look at /usr/sys/include/arch/alpha/builtins.h. For
UMULH it would be:

unsigned long __UMULH(unsigned long __oper1,
                         unsigned long __oper2);


 How about the IEEE floating point instructions with 
>rounding mode explicitly specified?  

Don't know.

>Do the compilers use the
>FETCH instruction when we are reading/writing a big array?

No. Since FETCH needs to support from the system (the 21064 and
21164 just send them off-chip) they aren't terribly useful in
any currently shipping system. The compilers *do* use the prefetch
instructions that have been added to the Alpha architecture with the
introduction of the 21164 and up (i.e. loads to R31 and F31).

>On the 21264, how will we access the population count
>and leading/trailing zero count instructions from Fortran and C?

Similar to UMULH, but not in current cc and f90 compilers since there
are no current operating system releases for 21264 machines. Expect
to see something akin to the other builtins there.

