The difference is one of programming ease in the years ahead. The "mixed"
camps are heading down the route that Windows went:

	mixed models

With Solaris 2.6 you already get -D_LARGEFILE_SOURCE -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64
compile switches and you likely have to track what libraries and
executables were built with 64 bit offsets and which ones with 32 bits.
It will be more fun when you also can get different pointer sizes in
Solaris 2.7. If you want to see what's ahead, look at Win16 and Win32:
	

	huge char *	
	char *

or somesuch nonsense, together with different opions in libraries and
headers of what they think the second argument to fseek(3s) is and
so on. You are right, 32/64 mixes operating systems are the way to
go.

