Mixed model programming is difficult, but it is even more
difficult when one side of the equation is a segmented 
16 bit memory model.  Writing a 16 bit application was
difficult in itself because of the fact all you had
was a 64k address space, and to get more than that you
had to resort to segment register manipulations.  That's
what makes Win16 programming difficult.

But if you are talking about programming ease, well the
thing would make programming easier is not having to 
convert an application that will never come close
to requiring 4GB of address space for code or data
into a 64 bit application.  Especially if you have
to allow the code to run on 32 bit machines that
don't have an option of running 64 bit binaries.
Otherwise you have to keep two versions of the code
or define a lot of C macros to get the right data
types and pointers in each version.  








