>
>Have you heard of I2O?  This new proprietary interface standard will 
>soon be putting the squeeze on free software.

I2O's silly license will restrict free OSes from distributing the sources
to the I2O driver, but will not restrict a binary distribution.

The cost of I2O membership is trival that every free OS group can afford
to join. I2O membership should not be rejected to free OS groups, else the
organization will get a visit from the FTC.

I think I2O's specifications being tied to agreements to not to distribute
the source -- even if your implementation was not based on the organization's
source collection is bad for everyone. Having to distribute an object-only
version of the I2O driver may rub free OS policy the wrong way, but it
will be done (even if it has to be done as a outside the distribution add-on)
if that is the only option.

I go as far as to agree with an end-user boycott of I2O hardware/software until
a more reasonable specification terms are available (not like I expect this to
change I2O's policy). The existing I2O specification terms are an irritant for
free OSes, but are not fatal to the future of the software. Nor is it fatal
to the support of I2O hardware.

--mark.



