In article <34690673.247F@nh.ultranet.com>, Mike <Mike@nh.ultranet.com> writes:
|>Just picked up a used VS 3100 M38 without a display. Got it for 
|>sentimental reasons.
|>

Nice little boxes ... mine just keep on chugging along ...

|>I have an RGB cable for it but, alas, no RGB monitor.
|>
|>Does someone have the pinout for the display connector ?
|>
|>Are there any suggestions as to interfacing with currently popular 
|>monitors ?
|>

I have an Iiyama Pro which can do RGB-sync-on-green, and currently on my
desk I have a Philips FSR871CV which can flick between RGB-sync-on-green and
VGA inputs so I can (and do) have two machines (a PC and a VS3100-76) but only
one monitor.

I have a note which indicates that the signals are:
	pin 1:  red
	pin 2:  colour ground
	pin 10: green
	pin 11: blue

If the above is wrong, find yourself a BC19S cable and have at it with a 
multimeter.

If your monitor already does sync-on-green you can probably just
build a suitable cable and off you go. If it is a typical PC SVGA monitor
with VGA style connector it will be expecting separate H- and V-sync signals.
The last time I needed this I managed to find a circuit on the Web which
claimed it could generate HH-/V-sync from sync-on-green: my need evaporated
before my soldering iron got involved and naturally I cannot find the circuit
so you'll have to ferret around a bit.

Of course, if you flip up S3 at the back of the box you can happily run
it as a non-Workstation using a terminal hooked up to the printer port as
a console. If you intend to run NetBSD, this is the best solution anyway
since there is no support for the graphics display (yet). If you intend to
pick up an OpenVMS hobbyist licence then you have some work to do to achieve
the full benefit.


