The issue was pointed out to me by a guy who had been in MCS getting complaints about
this for some time; the problem he reported was that, with the Microsoft C compiler,
a software FP package was being used by default instead of the hardware floating
point in the Alpha, leading to performance only ~10% better than Intel in that area. When
hardware floating point was used, the performance went up to where it should have
been. This was old news to some folks in VMS engineering who I spoke to about it at DECUS.
It may or may not still be the case, and the library (digital portable math lib) that uses hardware
floating point may now be used by default. This is not an issue of hardware, just a pointer
that IF your app under NT is getting less floating point oomph than you expected, check
that you are in fact using the hardware floating point.

I would be amazed if a Digital compiler would ever do such a thing; that was not the point.
glenn everhart


