Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> wrote:

It is not necessary to blow the fuse on the chips for NT only use. Arranging that some
of the boot structures are seen as corrupt when the early boot path parts of VMS
start to come in will keep VMS from running too, and leave it simpler to run VMS
for hardware debug or if decisions are made to sell a given box with VMS due to
some inventory or marketing issues. Such a firmware hack would be simple to reverse
(unlike the memory-mapping fuse in the chip) and could even be overcome by some secret
switch if anyone built such a switch in.  By the same token, early boot path code in Dec
Unix could be messed up by firmware switch if desired. The NT boot uses normally different
firmware.

Given that inventory issues and changes of mind about what OSs would be supported
HAVE occurred, it would be foolish to blow the "nt only" fuse in the Alpha. In fact, the
thing's existence could be viewed as posing a small risk to folks running VMS or DU
and could be very sensibly left out of newer generation chips. While it certainly existed
at one time, it may not even exist on the current generation Alphas. Firmware fiddling
can limit OS choice in a far less Draconian way.


