: 	Alpha's in their fabs (initially) not Hudson for a while.

I know nothing of Intel's process technology, nor am I aware of DEC's 
process technology.  As a matter of fact, I know very little about process
technology in general.  However, from what I do know, I know that you cannot
simply take a design from one design house, and plop it down to the fab
plant of your choosing, and have them fab it with the latest process
technology.  Design teams design these advanced processors with a specific 
processor technology as the target, and they try to make it scalable so
that it may be easily migrated from one generation to the next.  I don't 
think that you can just take an Alpha 21164 design over to Intel, and tell
Intel to fab it out of its Oregon plant.  Witness Motorola's inital problems
in fabbing the 604, as mismatching processor technology between it and 
IBM prevented Motorola from cranking out the high speed parts, and the 
high speed parts all came from IBM.  NexGen's NX686 was originally targeted 
for IBM's CMOS5X I believe, so when AMD acquired it, they couldn't just 
re layout the chip for AMD's 0.35um process, so AMD's fab plant is apparently 
trying to adapt the process technology with the original layout with some
problems. Yield is supposedly very low.  Just from remembering these 
stories leads me to believe that if Intel had to fab a 21164 in one of its 
fabs today, yield would be so notoriously low that Intel would have to charge 
both arms and both legs to sell it.  This may be especially true for Alpha, 
since Alphas run at such a high clock rate that the design tolerance is 
probably fairly low.

: 	Either way, Samsung is a TREMENDOUS positive Alpha influence as they
: 	have nothing to lose and everything to gain by getting .25 out the
: 	door earlier, forcing Intel to charge less than they may have wished,
: 	etc. etc.

: 				Rob




