From: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Hoff Hoffman)
Subject: The DIGITAL-Intel Deal, and Alpha...
Date: 1997/11/11
Message-ID: <64akhi$9v4@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 288707273
Distribution: world
Followup-To: comp.sys.dec
Organization: Digital Equipment Corp
Reply-To: hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms




  How can selling a fab line speed the schedule for speedier Alpha chips?

  DIGITAL's current and future Alpha microprocessors will continue to
  be produced by the semiconductor fabrication line (now, or soon to be)
  owned by Intel, and by lines owned by Samsung, by Mitsubishi, and
  (potentially) by other semiconductor vendors.  (For further information
  on Alpha microprocessor foundries, see http://www.AlphaPowered.com/.
  Also see the recent press coverage around the Samsung 700 MHz Alpha
  0.25 micron microprocessor.)

  This model of "outsourcing" of semiconductor fabrication is clearly
  used by the majority of other computer vendors -- and other than the
  folks at IBM, I am aware of no other computer system companies that
  run semiconductor fab lines with production volumes.  With the Intel
  agreement, DIGITAL will join these many other computer vendors that
  each use one or more outside semiconductor vendors to produce the
  necessary generic and custom semiconductor components.

  DIGITAL will maintain the design and engineering team that created
  Alpha and the current Alpha microprocessor designs, and the designers
  and engineers will continue working on future Alpha microprocessors.
  (More on this below...)  And -- per Robert Palmer's comments -- DIGITAL
  will continue manufacturing Alpha systems as long as there is a customer
  demand for them.

  The creation of, and the continuing process maintenance to, and the
  various necessary process upgrades to, a semiconductor fab line are
  all exceedingly expensive propositions -- I would not be surprised to
  hear the costs for the creation of a new fab line, using new technology,
  running into the billions of dollars.  With the DIGITAL-Intel agreement,
  DIGITAL does not have to invest in a new fab line, nor fab line updates.

  The Hudson (MA) "Fab 6" facility, the DIGITAL facility most directly
  involved with the recent DIGITAL-Intel agreement, is designed to produce
  0.35 and 0.25 micron semiconductor devices.  The Hudson fab is one of
  the fab lines that produces Alpha microprocessors -- it is not the only
  line.  (Again, see http://www.AlphaPowered.com/ for information.)

  With Mitsubishi, Samsung, and now Intel producing Alpha chips, DIGITAL
  expects to be able to produce newer Alpha chip designs more quickly
  in fab processes, such as 0.18 micron, than DIGITAL would have otherwise
  been able to.  (Various major semiconductor producers are presently
  moving to the newer 0.18 micron fabrication processes and fab lines,
  for various announced and/or planned semiconductor devices.)

  Jesse Lipcon's keynote speech at DECUS Anaheim 1997 included various
  additional bits of information on the Intel agreement -- in addition
  to some of the information included above.  Jesse also presented
  information on the roadmap for future Alpha microprocessors, and he
  indicated that the schedule for 0.18 micron Alpha microprocessors has
  been shortened by roughly a year -- and he stated this change in
  scheduling is a result of the recent Intel agreement.

  Also see http://www.digital.com/whats-new.html.

	--

  <The above is based on information available at DECUS, and from
  information at various public websites.  Any errors or misquotes
  in the above information are my own.>

