Rob Young wrote:
> 
>         So you see not only will the KP21264 be much cheaper than Merced,
>         it will also be shipping at least 9 months before Intel can cough
>         up a .25 micron part in Hudson.
> 
>         Prediction?

NOthing will happen. The same predictions were made over and over again
everytime Digital came up with a new generation chip. Alpha has been
around "forever" by chip standards and has not garnered a significant
part of the market as a whole.

Alpha has been shipping YEARS ahead of other 64 bit wannabes, and it
hasn't become popular.  If one model of Alpha, the 21264 ships a few
months before the big INTEL chip, it won't even make a dent in the
market as a whole.

It is a "NICHE" chip/architecture and will stay that way. Yes, it could
flourish,
but Bill Gates would have to oust Palmer and take over the helm of
Digital. It won't happen. It didn't happen when Palmer ousted Olsen and
it won't happen in the foreseable future either. Someone needs to clone
some marketing genes and inject them into DEC marketing droids. And
budgets must be allocated in the long term for some serious marketing.
And marketing includes low cost compilers, easy access to educational
markets as well as media advertising.

Also, and more importantly, the chip itself is but a fraction of the
cost of the 
"SYSTEM". The box as a whole, availability and cost of software,
maintenance costs, ease of doing business, programming/staff costs are
much more important then the cost of the "chip".


This is why Merced, even if the chip is more expensive, will be adopted
by the world. Simply because products based on that chip will be better
marketed, easier to buy, adn of course, there will be more software and
cheaper softwarte for it.

And while DEC may currrently have a monopoly on true 64 bit software,
there will be a tidal wave of ports of the WIN32 software into Win64 (or
whatever) that will quickly make the DEC software portfolio look like a
very poor kid on the block.



