At one session, I believe it was the "Digital Listens Panel," Jesse
Lipcon spent 30 minutes describing the settlement.

Essentially, as far as the Alpha chip is concerned, DEC is only, I
repeat ONLY contracting out the manufacturing of the chip itself.  It is
*no different* in that regard than the manufacturing agreements DEC
already has in place with Samsung and Mitsubishi.  DEC retains all of
the design teams, rights, and intellectual property associated with
Alpha.  Intel is only going to make the chips.  This "fabless" model has
been very successful for Sun, AMD, and Cyrix, also chipmakers without
manufacturing facilites.  For awhile, DEC even manufactured some of
AMD's chips.

Since DEC's "fabs" (semiconductor manufacturing facilities) were only
20-30% utilized, DEC couldn't afford the investments required to keep
them state-of-the-art.  Intel can keep them 100% busy, and thus afford
those investments.  Therefore, it's expected that EV6-7's 1.8 micron
development will be available at least one year earlier than if DEC had
not sold the fabs.

Industry and financial analysts are unanimous that this is a big win for
both Intel and DEC.

Jesse obviously said a lot more than this, but these are the main
points.

Mark

