In article <63orig$gru$1@ns.destek.net>, cherkus@unimaster.com (Dave Cherkus) writes:
> Matt Dillon (dillon@best.net) wrote:
> :     I think the only thing a 64 bit OS is good for is if you need
> :     to stick more then 4G of physical ram in the box.  There
> :     is a market for that, obviously, but not a huge one.
> 
> If this (and the rest of your article) is true, then how come Linux
> (which is a free operating system designed to run on inexpensive,
> single processor machines) runs in 64 bit mode on Alpha instead of 32
> bit mode (like VMS does)?  If your arguments are true, it would be
> cheaper, faster and satisfy more of its potential users.  If your
> arguments are so convincing, wouldn't someone have 'ported' it back to
> run in 32 bit mode to get these benefits?

The benefits VMS sees from having supported 32-bit addressing first
before going to 64-bit addressing have mainly to do with ease of
porting existing applications (primarily from VAX VMS).  DEC Unix
(nee DEC OSF/1) did not have any such encumbrance.

Although the OS support for 64-bit addressing arrived with V7.0,
the language support of 64-bit addressing is still only for the
three lowest level languages: Macro(64), Bliss, and C.

Larry Kilgallen



