"Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

> Since I was in Anaheim this last week for DECUS, I didn't see the
> November 3 issue of PC Week until late today. The lead article
> "Digital/Intel Settlement Portends Alpha Demise" is a quite a piece
> of work.
> 
> Well, a piece of SOMETHING, at any rate. What journalistic codswollop!

I agree, the following is what I fired off to PCWeek. 

-----

I was disappointed by the two articles about the Alpha cpu in your
November 3rd 97 issue. They seemed unbalanced and deliberately
negative towards the chip.

It is true that DEC have announced they will be supporting IA64, but
this is not actually news since before the lawsuit they were looking
at the architecture - those were the specifications Intel demanded
back. This was no surprise since DEC has been selling Intel based
systems for years and actually ships far more of them than Alpha based
systems. 

You state that "Digital becomes Intel's only customer for Alpha" and
claim this undermines the clone makers. In my own readings of the
various press releases this has not been mentioned but you may well
know something I don't. However in this article you neglect to mention
that the clone makers no longer need Digital to make the chips for
them as Samsung is now making the full range of current Alpha
chips. The 533MHz Samsung 21164PC low-cost chip for the volume
WindowsNT market is currently shipping and undercuts the 300MHz
Pentium II, the high-performance 600MHz 21164A for the high-end
WindowsNT and Unix markets will be in volume production by the end of
the year, and finally and most significantly the brand new 700MHz
21264A for next years super high-end, possibly the most powerful chip
ever made, is sampling now.

To give you credit where due, in another article you do mention this
Samsung development, but you call it "compelling competition for
Digital". This I think reveals a significant misunderstanding : DEC is
getting out of the fabrication business! Assuming the Intel deal goes
through so that DEC no longer manufactures Alphas, this is really
competition for Intel. It is quite an achievement for Samsung to have
gotten this far so quickly and it could well become the supplier of
the majority of Alphas. It is Intel that must play catchup now to get
the ex-Digital Hudson plant equipped to produce Alpha in 0.25 micron
silicon. I think it highly significant that the Alpha team is looking
at using the existing Intel 0.25 micron process - presumably the only
way for Intel to stay in the Alpha manufacturing game since there is
no proven DEC 0.25 micron process to rely upon.

Whilst I agree that IA64 and Merced will probably grow to become
dominant there is still a way to go. For example it should be
remembered that Merced will not fit onto even Intels most modern
current process (0.25 micron), and secondly that the architecture will
rely on completely new and challenging compiler technology to offer
the predicted performance, not something anyone can guarantee to get
right first time, on time. In the meanwhile DEC, Samsung and the Alpha
architecture will be making hay while the sun shines.

Chris Morgan <mihalis@ix.netcom.com>

