In article <3462A5CB.EEF@hiwaay.net>, Aaron 'AlphaMan' Sakovich <aaron@hiwaay.net> writes:

> Let's see -- Intel has to buy Digital's unused plant.  Intel has to 
> source it's competitor's processor.  Intel finally gets to lay claim
> to the fastest processor on the planet -- but it ain't theirs!!! 
> Intel doesn't control Alpha, it only shares in manufacturing with
> Samsung and Mitsubishi (rumour mill has a 4th fab coming online soon).
> Intel finally has to compete when selling a processor (Samsung last
> week announced a 700+ MHz 21264 processor).  Intel _must_ manufacture
> Alpha for 10 years.

I don't believe Intel is allowed to sell Alpha -- all the Alphas made
by Intel are for DEC.  The cross-licensing of patents means Intel could
design a processor quite similar (unlikely) but they are not allowed to
use DECs Alpha design to make chips and sell them on the open market.

Samsung and Mitsubishi are in a quite different category, although
if they succeed in a .<somethingsmaller> shrink of Alpha before Intel
does, it will be to Intel's discredit.  DEC could always elect to buy
from Samsung or Mitsubishi and even if they charge more than the price
DEC gets from the Intel agreement, it will still be quite less than DEC
charges customers for the completed systems.

Larry Kilgallen



