> Since Intel is only allowed to sell Alpha chips to is DEC, price
> comparisons seem irrelevant.  It is unlikely, however, that Samsung
> (or Mitsubishi) will sell Alpha chips to anyone for less than Intel
> sells them to DEC since the Intel prices were set under threat of a
> jury trial over patent infringement.
> 
> Larry Kilgallen
> 
> 	To me that reads like:
> 
> 		"Digital is unlikely to buy from Samsung since Samsung's
> 		prices are more likely to be higher than Intel's (due to
> 		patent agreement, etc.)."
> 
> 	Which really is a misphrasing of the question.  I should have
> 	asked:
> 
>  	And by the way, why should we assume Samsung's prices will be higher
> 	than Intel's?  A bad assumption here might be that Samsung will volume
> 	ship a .25 micron 21264 many months before Intel does (Since Hudson
> 	isn't ready for .25).  That "bad" assumption leads me to conclude
> 	that Intel can't afford to do that, THEREFORE Intel will manufacture
> 	Alpha's in their fabs (initially) not Hudson for a while.

I believe that the prices DEC (the sole customer for Intel Alpha chips)
pays Intel for Alpha chips are already set by the agreement which ended
the lawsuit.  Thus those prices are unaffected by Samsung pricing. In
my view there was enough intellectual property misappropriation involved
that DEC must get a _substantial_ discount from what chips go for on the 
open market.  Samsung is unlikely to be able to compete on price alone
when the customer is DEC.

When the customer is not DEC, Samsung may undercut DEC prices on the sale
of Alpha chips, and if they do that is not horrible for DEC as multiple
sources are good for DEC and help sell compilers, etc. as each Alpha ships.

> 	Either way, Samsung is a TREMENDOUS positive Alpha influence as they
> 	have nothing to lose and everything to gain by getting .25 out the
> 	door earlier, forcing Intel to charge less than they may have wished,
> 	etc. etc.

Samsung enthusiasm is great for Alpha and thus for DEC, but...

I believe Intel has no leeway on pricing of sales to DEC (the only Alpha
customer they are allowed).  Certainly if DEC starts buying the (presumably
more expensive to them due to not being subsidized by a lawsuit settlement)
Samsung chips it is a statement about relative quality from the two chip
foundries.

Or perhaps the price DEC pays to Intel for Alpha chips is indexed to the
price the world pays Intel for IA64 chips, but if that were the case Intel
would be unlikely to shift their price for IA64 just to "compete" on the
price for what is a relatively lower-profit item for them.

Larry Kilgallen



