Just to flesh out this thread about OS, prices a bit more, I'm including 
here the prices to buy the minimal set of OpenVMS licenses needed to 
transform an Alphaworkstation into a machine that you can actually log into
and have a screen to work with, and comparing these to real prices for 
otherOS's.  The OpenVMS configuration is is a very minimal workstation
configuration (no compilers, no application software, just the window
environment, the OS, and a single user license).  These prices date from
February 1997, and I don't know if they have changed since then. (These were
for an alphastation 200 4/166, which I saw advertised at the time for
$2250, but no licenses.) 

  QL-MTAE-6L   (VMS base)     845  (Otherwise, zip)
  QL-MT3AA-3B  (single user)  327  (Otherwise, can't do squat)
  QL-36NAE-AA  (NAS 150)     1770  (Otherwise, no DECwindows, no net)
  total                      2942  <---!!!!!!!!

Compare that to a Solaris 2 year subscription (for Intel OR Sparc),

  desktop   $495  (2 interactive users)
  server   $2895  (unlimited interactive users, plus other goodies)

or to the largest OS/2 server license, which used to be $1300

or to WNT prices (from Egghead software, probably available for less elsewhere)

  WNT Workstation 4.0 (full product)  299.91  
  WNT Server 4.0  w/5 clients (full)  589.22

In a nutshell, Digital charges more for a simple workstation license for
OpenVMS than any of these competitors do for a full server license.  When
comparing "desktop" prices, OpenVMS costs at least 6X as much as do these
other options.  In fact, it is much worse than even this factor, since the
Solaris option includes upgrades, and the OpenVMS license does not, and the
others are even less expensive than Solaris.  Wave hands a bit to take
these and other factors into account, and OpenVMS desktop licenses cost
about 8-9X more *just for the software*, we're not even including the cost
of the workstation.

Given this huge price discrepancy for base licenses it will be interesting
to see what Digital charges for the new alphaPC based OpenVMS workstations,
assuming that they ever get around to shipping them. (Amazing how Digital
always seems to have something a bit more pressing to do than get these out
the door - probably something in the fine print of their Alliance with
Microsoft - right alongside the section prohibiting them from discussing
this clause in court.)  If Digital holds true to form, and current prices,
they will price these alphaPCs with OpenVMS $1500 to $2000 more per unit
than the WNT versions for the exact same machine, a relative markup that
will be absolutely staggering for machines in this price range, and it
will likely reduce sales to the negligible level that Digital seems to
expect.

Still, we can pray for a miracle, and maybe just for once they will
surprise us and price OpenVMS systems competitively (that is, they
shouldn't cost more than a couple of hundred dollars more with OpenVMS than
with WNT.) Then if we get a second miracle, and they reduce the price of
the compilers into the realm where mere mortals can afford them, well, it
could be a brave new world. 

Of course, speaking realistically, the odds are better that Elvis will lead
a hostile takeover at the November stockholders meeting than that we will
see competitive OpenVMS pricing from this incarnation of Digital :-(. 

Regards,

