Organization: Flying Disk Systems, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms


I was quite amused to learn this weekend that Intel Pentium processors
will crash (actually, hang completely, requiring a reboot)
when a particular 4-byte illegal instruction sequence
(F0 0F C7 C8) is executed in *user* mode.   Imagine your reaction
if you had learned that your VAX or Alpha CPU had a user-mode
CRASH instruction that the operating system could not protect
against!   This is hacker field day.

This means that a *large* number of multi-user Pentium systems
(ISPs, Universities, etc.) are at risk from denial-of-service
attacks.   While I certainly sympathize with their system managers,
I can't help but chuckle smugly for sticking with DEC.

It will be interesting to see what percentage of these sites
upgrade to Alpha systems, as opposed to Cyrix or AMD (who
properly trap it as an illegal instruction).

For those who are not familiar with this new Pentium bug, see
the discussion in comp.sys.intel.   The original note was posted
on 07-Nov-1997, but there are already well over 250 replies.

