Terry C. Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> wrote:
>And DEC should have Alphas running at 1GHz within less than two years.
>
>Fastest clock speeds I've seen thus far on Alpha roadmaps is 1200MHz,
>and that's for 2000 or 2001 delivery. Based on the firm's track record
>to date, I have little doubt that they can make good on their promise.

That will be very interesting.  If the clock speeds ever go above 2000 Mhz,
then the Alpha processors could also function as microwave ovens to keep
food and coffee warm. :-)

More seriously, in the worst-case scenario for Digital, it will be
prohibited from exporting its fastest chips in large quantities based
on an arbitrary MTOPS (Maximum Theoretical Operations Per Second) export
restriction that has reportedly passed the House and Senate.  If this
export restriction becomes law, then Digital can wave goodbye to its
Samsung and Mitsubishi fab partners for the production of the fastest
chips in the future and become more dependent on Intel for domestic
high-end Alpha chip production.  Legitimate production problems can
creep into the production of Alpha chips on bleeding-edge process
technology, and even if demand for the fastest Alpha processors becomes
high in the United States, Intel may not have the ability or the
greatest enthusiasm to meet that demand while remaining profitable for
itself.  Digital could sue all they want, but in the meantime, they
would be bleeding money.  That is not a pretty picture.  Let us hope
that events do not happen that way.

-Ed L



