You are leaving out hubris.

Basically, Linux is a 64-bit port because it is a 64-bit CPU.  Kind
of makes sense, doesn't it?

Plus, Linux is a bit more efficient with memory than DU.  Putting Linux on
an Alpha here where I work was a huge eye opener into DU inneficiencies.
The speed and tremendously lower memory use are definitely nice.  

Anyway, I don't think he meant to put 64-bit down or say it wasn't
useful.  But his points were good in that most people really don't need
64-bits and it can be detrimental rather than beneficial.  So much depends
on what you need and what kind of software you are running.

As far as why they didn't port back to 32-bits, I'm not sure what you are
trying to say.  The one thing that makes me want 32-bits on my Alpha right
now is memory use.  It is pretty dismal.  But I doubt anyone would spend
the time/money on such a port when memory is so cheap.  Most of the
problems I have with memory are DU's handling of memory and that pitiful
NCR SCSI controller.

Only DEC would sell a $15K workstation with a $100 SCSI controller.

