In article <63l4g8$8mg@gap.cco.caltech.edu>
mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu wrote:

> In article <63d177$ri9@flonk.uk.sun.com>, Andrew Harrison SUNUK Consultancy 
<andrew.harrison@uk.sun.com#> writes:
>>Remind me what the potential Digital strategy was (porting DU to IA64)guess
>>what that will do to DU on Alpha sales, what happens if OpenVMS is
>>ported to IA64.
> 
> Exactly!  Digital made that exact same mistake during the VAX to Alpha
> transition.  They announced the upcoming Alphas long before they shipped,
> and managed to tank VAX sales before there were any Alphas around to sell.
> It would probably have been much better if they kept their yaps shut about
> all OS ports from Alpha->Merced, and then just sprung them when they were 
> ready.  Ok, it would piss off the folks who bought the alpha the week 
> before, but it's a lot better for business if the customers say, "hey,
> I can now buy that DU/intel machine", rather than "hey, I'm going to wait
> a couple of years until the DU/intel machines come out."  One is an 
> immediate sale, the other is the loss of an immediate sale, with *maybe* a 
> real sale down the line.
> 
> It still looks to me as though the DU crowd has too much influence within
> the company.  I doubt that many people outside of the company give DU a
> realistic chance of surviving in the crowded Merced Unix field.  I mean, it
> is a tiny player in the current Unix market, and that even with certain
> hardware advantages.  So they'll spend a fortune porting it, the market
> will never develop, and then where are they?  At best just another clone
> WNT vendor.
> 
> Conversely, if they do port OpenVMS, it might at least be interesting since
> OpenVMS is not just another Unix.  People buy OpenVMS for reliability
> mostly, and knocking a few percent off the top end speed in return for
> cheaper hardware wouldn't be that bad a tradeoff.  (If they were really
> bright about it they would make the port so that it could run on any
> Merced, and then sell the OS to anybody who wanted it, as Microsoft does
> with WNT.)  While I consider Digital's inability to market the Alpha a sign
> of complete and total corporate incompetence, the fact is, that in terms of
> getting my work done, I really don't care what's under the hood, if the
> speed is adequate, the OS is OpenVMS, and the application software is
> available.  That last one is the sticking point - after half a decade of
> corporate neglect, and sometimes outright hostility, I suspect that most
> companies would not port their existing OpenVMS/Alpha software to Merced,
> choosing instead to move it to either WNT or Solaris/HP/AIX (assuming that
> they had not already done so.) 
> 
> So summing up, DU moves to Merced, and gets no market share (can't
> compete). OpenVMS moves to Merced, but Digital has badmouthed it to death,
> nobody makes software for it, and the Merced market for it never develops
> either.  This is a really odd scenario, but it looks like Digital could
> conceivably port two OS's from Alpha to Merced, and then find that for all
> that work and investment, the only products that they are still selling are
> the Alpha versions!  I guess we should take (cold) comfort in knowing that 
> these factors will keep the Alpha support around a lot longer than Digital
> would otherwise have planned.
> 
> What a mess! 
> 
> Regards,
> 
> David Mathog
> mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu
> Manager, sequence analysis facility, biology division, Caltech 
> **************************************************************************
> *   Affordable VMS? See:  http://seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu/ww/pcvms.html   *
> **************************************************************************

So why would someone want VMS on a, say, 50 SPECmark chip in 1999 when they
can have it on a 150 SPECmark chip? A port to a slower Merced chip seems an odd
thing to want in that light...





