In article <34613539.4E4C@tmfeng.mv.com>
Dave Marra <marra@tmfeng.mv.com> wrote:

> Jim Ross wrote:
>> 
>> The VS 4000 VLC has 6 memory sockets for what looks like standard 72 pin
>> SIMMs.  Are they standard SIMMs or some weird DEC part?
>> 
>> I heard that it can only take 4MB SIMMs, thus it's limited to 24MB.  Can
>> it take larger SIMMs?
>> 
>> What is the fastest speed the onboard modem terminal port can operate at?
>> 
>> j
> 
> What you have heard is correct.  They are NOT standard SIMMS, and, the
> machine is limited to 24mb.
> 
> 						.dave.
>
What I heard while at DEC was that they ARE standard SIMMs. They must be
4MB parts, with parity, 70 nsec. or faster. Admittedly parity SIMMs are not
quite the usual PC "standard" but they are widely available. The machine
does indeed max out at 24MB. Remember the VLC was built with lots of PC
type parts to get the cost down.

Just be sure to get parity SIMMs and that they really are 70ns or faster. Some
PC parts for 70ns have been reported to be a tad slow...

Faster SIMMs should be just fine.

glenn everhart






