In article <345B574D.5A98@bbc.co.uk>, Tim Llewellyn <tim.llewellyn@bbc.co.uk> writes:
> Can anyone give me some starting clues about the options
> available for retrofitting Raid to some VAX 4000-100 machines.
> Currently we have mostly DSSI disks (mainly RF35 and RF72)
> plus a BA35x with HSD05 DSSI to SCSI controller and one RZ28
> in the BA35x. There are 3 free slots in the BA35x and buying
> more Storeageworks disks might also be an option.
> 
> We lost a system disk (RF35) last week so maybe the management
> might just take notice now of my recommendations from several
> months back now. 

If you're worrying about data redundancy, you don't want RAID 0, which is
striping.  RAID 1 is mirroring, aka volume shadowing.

> The unix systems on the project have raid 10 for the system
> disk and raid 5 for the data. 

I have not heard of RAID 10. You sure this isn't RAID 0+1 (ie a mirrored stripe
set)?

> Is there controller based RAID options for the VAX 4000-100,
> or just hist based?

You can certainly do host-based volume shadowing (HBVS).  This is efficient,
and you can now license it by disk or by node.  We run it here, on big as well
as little Vaxes and Alphas.  We've used it since 1990, and have VERY critical
data survive a disk failure.  The first time we lost a shadow set member, it
took us 3 days to notice it.  That shadow set had our cluster-wide SYSUAF on
it.  Without volume shadowing, we would have taken a prime-time cluster outage
affecting thousands of users.  I think we would have noticed that in under 3
days :-)

HBVS requires like disks - ie, you can only shadow an RF35 with another RF35. 

There are a bunch of controller-based options as well from multiple vendors. 
Most of these are SCSI-based (SCSI to the host as well as to the drives).  I
don't think there is anything preventing you from plugging one of these boxes
into your HSD05. Check with CMD (they designed your HSD05) for the solutions
they're currently offering, as well as with Digital.  Then price out the
differences and see which is cheaper in the long run.  Don't forget to count
the software maintenance costs for HBVS, since it isn't cheap.

	.../Ed
