SGI_Origin_2000_2            4    666.0    727.0    792.0    874.0
SGI_Origin_2000_2            2    351.0    365.0    392.0    413.0
Sun_UE_10000                63   6307.0   6391.0   7203.0   7197.0
Sun_UE_10000                48   5961.0   6056.0   6861.0   6914.0
Sun_UE_10000                32   4397.0   4408.0   5166.0   5188.0
Sun_UE_10000                16   2371.0   2414.0   2942.0   2905.0
Sun_UE_10000                 8   1271.0   1270.0   1544.0   1546.0
Sun_UE_10000                 1    164.0    164.0    202.0    202.0
Sun_UE_6001                 12   1785.0   1750.0   1884.0   1848.0
Sun_UE_6001                  8   1517.0   1528.0   1371.0   1501.0
Sun_UE_6001                  4    921.0    900.0    796.0    891.0
Sun_UE_6001                  1    281.0    281.0    224.0    261.0
Sun_SC2000E                 20    426.9    364.8    456.2    408.2
Sun_SC2000E                 16    330.2    289.5    351.1    323.2
Sun_SC2000E                 12    237.2    214.3    257.6    240.9
Sun_SC2000E                  8    140.9    131.9    153.1    147.8
Sun_SC2000E                  4    111.6    118.8    128.8    125.7
Sun_SC2000E                  2     66.4     53.6     57.0     70.1
Sun_SC2000E                  1     32.2     29.5     36.5     33.4


What is notable is that there are SGI machine that indeed get a collective
bandwidth that is 21857.6/1026.7 = 21 times as high as the highest measured
Turbolaser number, and there is one SUN machine that gets 6307.0/1026.7 = 6
times as fast, you will also note that for similar number of processors the
differences aren't that big:

DEC_8400_5-350               8   1026.7    986.1   1065.0   1132.3
SGI_Origin_2000_2            8   1355.0   1450.0   1413.0   1675.0
Sun_UE_6001                  8   1517.0   1528.0   1371.0   1501.0
Sun_SC2000E                  8    140.9    131.9    153.1    147.8

The SGI system doesn't have a "backplane" in the usual sense, the SUN
10000 also has a crossbar but relatively poor single CPU bandwidth:

DEC_8400_5-350               1    215.7    207.5    219.6    234.2
Sun_UE_10000                 1    164.0    164.0    202.0    202.0
Sun_UE_6001                  1    281.0    281.0    224.0    261.0

The SUN 6000 has good single CPU bandwidth, but isn't exactly "an order
of magnitude faster" in it's backplane overall:

DEC_8400_5-350               8   1026.7    986.1   1065.0   1132.3
Sun_UE_6001                 12   1785.0   1750.0   1884.0   1848.0

I don't know why Andrew included HP in his list, because there are no
measured numbers for any of their systems that are worth writing home about.

Again, the whole point that Andrew was trying to make, i.e. that backplane
bandwidth is limiting TPC-C is wrong (he should ask his own engineers
about hooking up a bus monitor to see whether my statement is true). There
*are* important workloads where backplane bandwidth is tantamount, but
TPC-C responds nicely to caches.

