>
>Careful of blanket statements like that.  You might want to try running
>your numbers again on a 5000/260 (MIPS R4400 based) DecStation versus
>an early entry level Alpha (3000-300L for example). From what I recall
>the MIPS-based DECStation compares fairly favorably.

I said:

	All our labs Alphas (none of which is as slow as a 3000/300L
	which is the most crippled of all Alphas ever built with
	the possible exception of the Multia) compared to my
	box (a 5000/240).

But even then, let's have a look at the performance of the two machines
you mentioned:

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
System            CPU        ClkMHz  Cache      SPECint SPECfp  Info  Source
Name              (NUMx)Type ext/in  Ext+I/D      92      92    Date  Obtained
================= ========== ======= ========== ======= ======= ===== =========
DEC 3000/300L     A21064     20/100  256+8/8      45.9    63.6  Apr93 c.sun.mc
DEC 5000/260      R4400      60/120  1M+16/16     57.1    54.5  Sep93 c.arch
DEC 3000/300LX    A21064     25/125  256+8/8      63.5    75.5  May94 SPEC news
DEC 5000/240      R3000      40      64/64        27.9    35.8  Jun93 DECinfo
DEC 3000/500      A21064     30/150  512+8/8      84.4   127.7  Apr93 c.arch

The slowest machine we have in the lab is a 3000/300LX. Measurements with
the SPEC benchmarks show that MXed images could get about 80% of the speed
of the native Alpha images produced by the then current Alpha compilers
(ca. 1993, i.e. not as good as today) on SPECint92 and 65% on SPECfp92.
So I would get

	63.5 * 0.8 = 50.8	SPECint92
	75.5 * 0.65 = 49.0	SPECfp92

on our 3000/300LX, quite a bit faster than my 5000/240 and not much slower
even than the 5000/260 you quote (12%). For the non-crippled Alphas of the
time (i.e. those that had a 128 bit cache path, the 3000/300 series had
only a 64 bit cache interface), the MX numbers would beat the 5000/260
handily:

	84.4 * 0.8 = 67.5
	127.7 * 0.65 = 83

So I stand by my statement that at least Digitals MIPS-based machines were
not competitive in performance with Alpha's running MIPS-code translated.

