>SGI and HP have backplanes an order of magnitude faster,

Funny you should say that, but do you know that the *backplane* isn't what
is limiting TPC-C performance on the Turbolaser ? Hint: If it were, why
can we get such great scaling in clusters with "only" 100 Mbyte of memory
channel cross section bandwidth ? Backplane bandwidth is important for
some applications, but as long as it's reasonably fast, it's not the
limiter for TPC-C. I leave it to Andrew to pick the *real* limiter
in the Turbolaser, because of course he is right that *something* is keeping
the machine from fully exploiting TPC-C here. 

Note to readers: If all your database workloads are so perfectly parallel
like TPC-C, go with a vendor with lots of slower CPU's or better still
use lots of smaller cheaper database servers. If it's more realistic with
hotspots that go sequential on single CPUs from time to time, give a 
Turbolaser a try.

>...selling futures, you can only get revenue for actual products.

A pity yes. At least we can now start selling 21264 upgrades to Turbolasers,
which will prove my statement that the backplane is not the limiter for
TPC-C (which is not to say that we don't need to revamp the ageing backplane
of our highest end offering). Heck, even the low end 21264 desktop systems next
year have 2-3 times the backplane bandwidth of a Turbolaser.
