Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:

>Yes - but that's not too surprising.  OpenVMS - in anything like its
>current incarnation - simply won't run on a generic
>processor.  The Alpha requires PALcode to be loaded into it before
>it'll run VMS, in particular.

PALcode is nothing more than an abstraction layer; there's nothing
special about it. In fact, the large majority of it--on some
machines, all of it--is all implemented using standard generic
Alpha machine code. There'd be no problem moving some of the
machine-dependent stuff up out of PALcode and into the lower levels
of an OS to get it to run, assuming you had the machine-specific
technical information to write code that does what the PALcode
does.

>Unless such a provision is in the IA-64,
>it won't run real OpenVMS.

From my knowledge of how the Alpha works and how OpenVMS interfaces
with it (gleaned from a couple of readings of the Alpha Microprocessor
Handbook, including the VMS PALcode section) I just don't see why
this would be the case.

