jf mezei wrote:
> 
> Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz wrote:
> > If there were any advantage for VMS on IA-64, we might even try it,
> > though it would be a lot more difficult to port it than NT or Digital
> > Unix. Why should we port VMS to a slower processor ?
> 
> While IA64 may not have ~any~ technical advantages over Alpha, it will
> have one unbeatable asset: MARKETING. It will also have products that
> are lower priced do to extreme competition and the fact that there will
> be so many players will also give it more visibility.

  The collection of recent advances in computer architecture that HP and
  Intel have named EPIC do offer advantages in high performance CPU design
  over classic RISC designs like Alpha.  The crucial question is: is this
  advantage as great as the advantage classic RISC has over CISC designs
  like x86?  The answer is probably not.

  This might lead to an interesting role reversal for classic RISC designs.
  In the past RISC has enjoyed technical superiority but the entrenched
  market leader was able to steal RISC implementation tricks and drive
  process technology ahead fastest. This allowed CISC CPU performance to
  stay close enough to RISC to reduce temptation for users to discount
  their huge software investment in x86 and jump to RISC on the desktop.

  Now RISC is the entrenched champion in high end servers and technical
  workstations.  Will DEC and Sun be able to emulate Intel's successful
  strategy and aggressively push classic RISC designs forward and stay
  within spitting distance (or even somewhat ahead) of IA-64 CPUs?  The
  two main flaws in this theory are 1) DEC and Sun are fabless and are
  hostage to others in keeping their CPUs in the bleeding edge of process
  technology, 2) the RISC software base is less overwhelming than the
  x86 code base and far more portable and so represents a lower potential
  barrier to customers jumping ship.  So RISC vendors hold fewer cards
  than Intel did but maybe this is balanced off against a smaller inherent
  disadvantage of RISC to IA-64.

  The entertaining part of all this will be the "trading places" in the
  marketing battles that will occur when Merced finally appears on the
  scene:

  Early 1990s:

            CISC forces                    RISC forces

    RISC code is too big and        RISC has technical superiority.
    bloated.                        CISC has too few registers.
    Users have years of             RISC has less baggage than CISC.
    experience tuning CISC          RISC can better take advantage of
    applications.                   newer semi processes.
    CISC CPUs can use "RISC         RISC is the new wave of computing
    techniques" to keep up.         CISC just cannot keep up with RISC.
    CISC compilers work fine        RISC has far better floating point
    with just 8 gp registers.       performance than CISC.
    RISC benchmark advantage        CISC is too complex too for high
    disappears for system level     clock rates.
    performance.                    RISC is a better target for modern
    RISC is too expensive and       optimizing compilers.
    needs huge caches.


  Now fast forward to early 2000s:

            RISC forces                    EPIC forces

    EPIC code is too big and        EPIC has technical superiority.
    bloated.                        RISC has too few registers.
    Users have years of             EPIC has less baggage than RISC.
    experience tuning RISC          EPIC can better take advantage of
    applications.                   newer semi processes.
    RISC CPUs can use "EPIC         EPIC is the new wave of computing.
    techniques" to keep up.         RISC just cannot keep up with CISC.
    RISC compilers work fine        EPIC has far better 3D/multi-media
    with just 31 gp registers.      performance than RISC.
    EPIC benchmark advantage        RISC superscalar is too complex for
    disappears for system level     high clock rates.
    performance.                    EPIC is a better target for modern
    EPIC is too expensive and       optimizing compilers.
    needs huge caches.


  I have already seen signs of these arguments by both sides of the
  EPIC debate.  The biggest difference in these two debates is that
  CISC proponents pointed out that RISC instructions have lower sem-
  antic content than CISC instructions and the need to execute more
  RISC instructions to just keep up.  It is hard to see an analogous
  advantage for RISC in the RISC vs EPIC debate.


All opinions strictly my own.

--
Paul W. DeMone                 The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race
Kanata, Ontario                to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com              architectures with MIPSed results but
PaulDeMone@EasyInternet.net    ALPHA's well that ends well.




