jf mezei <"[non-spam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca> writes:

> You mean to tell me that a lousy 8086 ugly architecture that should have
> been restricted to toasters and games went on to outspeed the VAX
> because the VAX was such a poor design ?

Just so. The VAX is a nice design, but it's its orthogonality and generality
that gets in the way for fast implementations. Just look at the things a TLB
and MMU have to do for a worst-case instructions such as ADDP6, or even just
one of the arithmetic instructions using some of the more involved addressing
modes. Compared to that, a x86 or 370 instruction set implementation is easy...

> I am sure that had Digital dropped its plans for ALPHA and focused on
> VAX, it would probably have been able to get the same improvements on
> the VAX chip that Intel was able to squeeze out of the 8086. Digital
> invested instead on ALPHA so it is to be extected that VAX speed
> increases stopped because the reasearch went to ALPHA.

Nope, the NVAX and NVAX+ chips, running at up to 100MHz, IIRC, came out more
or less contemporaneously with the 21064.

	Jan



