| Exactly!  Digital made that exact same mistake during the VAX to Alpha
| transition.  They announced the upcoming Alphas long before they 
| shipped, and managed to tank VAX sales before there were any Alphas 
| around to sell....

This is nonsense.

VAX sales dropped precipitously well before Alpha was public knowledge. 
I was at DECWORLD'90 as an ISV, having left DEC the year before.  That
show represent DEC at its peak.  However, if you talked to the sales
guys as an insider - and I still knew enough of them to get into that
kind of discussion - a very different picture emerged:  Of a sudden,
sharp dropoff in VAX orders in the preceding quarter.  This was also the
time of the 9000 introduction.  A bit of the dropoff came from
anticipation of the 9000's, but actual demand for them never
materialized.

At DECWORLD'90, there was still a great deal of enthusiasm.  Over the
following year, it became quite clear that interest in developing new
applications on VMS had dried up.

By the time DEC started talking about Alpha, it had little choice: 
Sales had tanked, as customers were disappointed with 9000's (remember
the projections in the business press about the billions of dollars the
9000 was going to contribute to DEC's bottom line?) and saw their VAXes
as slow, expensive machines compared to the available RISC-based systems
that had captured everyone's attention - hell, even *DEC* was using the
MIPS RISC chip for its workstations.  Customers need to plan, and
vendors need to let them know what direction their planning should take.
Alpha was to be *the* major strategic direction for DEC.  How could they
*not* talk about it?

BTW, the effect you describe - announcing a new produce kills demand for
the current one, severely damaging the vendor - is usually called the
"Osbourne effect", after Osbourne Computer, an early maker of personal
machines that pre-announced a "version 2" of its machine that was so
much better than the current "version 1" that all sales of version 1
machines immediately stopped.  Unfortunately, version 2 still need
plenty of work, and when the cash flow from version 1 stopped, the
company went bankrupt.

It's a great, even true, story, but it's almost unique.  It also has
nothing to do with DEC's history.  DEC at the time had plenty of cash,
plenty of other business (software, service, etc. - Osbourne had *only*
the sales of its version 1 machines to live off of) and could have
easily survived a complete cessation of VAX sales for the time it needed
to complete and ship Alpha.  In fact, VAXes continued to sell (at the
abysmally low rate that they'd been selling for months).  At Alpha's
introduction, DEC was still (financially) a healthy company, and had
generated a huge amount of publicity and interest.  (It then proceeded
to drop the ball, repeatedly, all over its toes, in oh so many ways; but
that's another (very sad) story.)
							-- Jerry



