jf mezei wrote:
> 
> Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz wrote:
> > >> It was both. The fastest VAXes ever built are slower than a Pentium Pro.
> 
> > Design. A VAX is even worse to pipeline or make fast than a x86. It
> > is the epitome of CISC.
> 
> You mean to tell me that a lousy 8086 ugly architecture that should have
> been restricted to toasters and games went on to outspeed the VAX
> because the VAX was such a poor design ?

   Ironic isn't it!  VAX was designed when microcoded, serial CPU impl-
   ementions were nearly universal.  VAX wasn't a bad design at that time.
   It was very clean, attractive, and wasn't particulary onerous compared
   even to earlier, simpler architectures (compare VAX-11/780 vs PDP-11/40).
   For complex, heavily pipelined VLSI implementations with millions of
   transistors to throw at multiple instruction decode and issue per clock,
   the VAX ISA sucks.

   The VAX was designed to be the assembly programmers dream with orthogonal
   availability of a multitude of addressing modes with 1, 2, and 3 address
   instructions.  It was a palatial overindulgence that relied heavily on
   both a serial instruction decoding process and the extensive use of
   microcode to support its heavy edifice.

   The 8086 couldn't aspire to such excess because it was crash designed in
   a short period of time and had to be implemented with minimal hardware
   resources (under 30,000 transistors).  This forced relative simplicity
   was actually a blessing in disguise to help exploit future advances in
   implementation technology.  Also x86 implementations have been pushed
   really hard because the payoff in catering to its locked in software base
   is mindboggling and justifies the huge investment in complex, painstakingly
   crafted and laid out designs built in bleeding edge fab facilities.  Intel
   has actually rolled out many implementation features ahead of RISC vendors
   in its race to keep x86 integer performance within a factor of 50-70% of
   leading RISC devices (clock rate doubling, dual ported L1 data cache, two
   bit branch prediction, multi level branch prediction, low voltage swing bus
   interface, aggressive OOO execution etc)

> 
> I am sure that had Digital dropped its plans for ALPHA and focused on
> VAX, it would probably have been able to get the same improvements on
> the VAX chip that Intel was able to squeeze out of the 8086. Digital
> invested instead on ALPHA so it is to be extected that VAX speed
> increases stopped because the reasearch went to ALPHA.

   Fat chance.  The writing was on the wall for VAX given the incredible
   advantage RISC offered for the technical and midrange computing apps
   that VAX had owned, and the relatively portable software base.  Heck,
   VAX couldn't effectively keep up with the Motorola 680X0 line just in
   performance (let alone price/performance) even before RISC showed up.

   To see the incredible difference in performance versus hardware costs
   between VAX and RISC compare the VAX 8800 vs the HP 9000 model 840, the
   first HP PA-RISC machine shipped as a commercial product.  Both machines
   offered similar performance (roughly 8 MIPS) but the 8800 was an very
   expensive machine being implemented with custom ECL gate arrays while
   the HP was slapped together out of commercial, off-the-shelf TTL parts.

   The clearest example showing the limitation inherent to the VAX ISA is
   the NVAX vs the Alpha 21064.  Both were implemented in the same 0.75 um
   CMOS process with roughly a million and a half transistors.  The VAX chip
   eventually topped off at 171 MHz yielding 60 MIPs and 77 SPEC89 while the
   21064 reached 200 MHz giving 215 MIPS and 184 SPEC89.

