Continuing this discussion is probably one of those pointless
excercises, 
so this will be my last post on the subject. 

The situation is really more complex than this. I've personally had 
extremely irate customers who wanted UNIX (which is, by the way, my
primary area of expertise) go ballistic when we pitched VMS as a better
solution, because we were perceived as "trying to trap them into buying
proprietary products". I've had VMS shops run extensive benchmarks
on competitor UNIX gear, then go ballistic because we also proposed a
UNIX solution, thereby "proving our lack of commitment to VMS" - then
buy from Sun or HP. Sometimes, you really can't present the whole range
of available solutions, because people don't view this stuff
objectively.
There are emotional biases, and if you don't see them, you get bitten.
So
many times, sales reps take the path of least resistance - you propose 
what you think is acceptable. If a customer is looking at NT, you
probably
propose NT. The same for UNIX. If a customer is interested in the
excellent
features of VMS, you propose that, as well, but you always have to be 
careful about being viewed as trying to lure the customer into some
locked-in proprietary scheme.

Sometimes you can't make people happy. Any commitment to any of VMS,
UNIX, or NT is perceived negatively by proponents of the the other two
camps. And it need not be that way. The products often fill different
needs, and and should be used appropriately. For instance, much has 
been made of the fact that desktop VMS is not aggresively pursued by
Digital. Even if Digital did so, where do you think you would get
cost-effective alternatives to Microsoft Office and the myriad other
applications that a desktop needs?  The days when a single company can
provide the whole soup-to-nuts gamut of software that every 
organization needs is gone. The industry is too competitive, and the
development costs are too high.  So use VMS servers for your critical
computing, or Digital UNIX for your high-performance computing or 
data warehousing projects. Use NT workstations for CAD or general 
office productivity tools. It's not a one size fits all world anymore.
That's what open, standards based computing is all about, right?
You just need to make sure that when you buy all this stuff, you can
find a vendor that can help you bolt it all together. And I still
think Digital is better suited for that than any other company.

> Alternately they should provide all the information in an easily
> accessible form. N.B.  "provide", not merely have on hand, /minimally/
> let the customer know that the information is there.
> 
> The current sales reps largely don't know diddly about VMS so they
> can't do their job properly.
> 
As to the lack of VMS expertise, I can only speak to that both in terms
of the company's stated policy, and by personal experience. Within the 
area where I live and work, all the remaining Digital sales folks are
long-timers, with extensive VMS sales experience. And a huge percentage
of the third-party reps are ex-Digital folks, also with large amounts 
of VMS experience. 

And I'll re-iterate: Sales folks don't have any vested interest in
selling
one solution over another. They make their money either way. And there
is NO
corporate directive to de-emphasize VMS. We try sell what customers
want.
Sometimes it's hard to figure out what that is, though.

Again, this stuff is my own opionion, and should not be construed as an
official statement by Digital Equipment Corporation.

Thanks,
Eric D. Osborne



