>"David Mathog " <mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu> wrote:
>Maybe it would help if DEC stopped thinking of its products as computers
>and thought of them instead as breakfast cereal.  DEC Crunchies are currently
>selling at $10/box, and Microsoft/Intel flakeys are selling at $2/box.
>DECs cereal tastes a bit better, and it has a longer shelf life, but
>who's going to spend the extra $8/box for what is essentially the same
>products

Jerome Fine replies:

It's possible that the real difficulty lays much further back than
most people realize.  While I must admit that I really like DECs
concepts in software and their hardware CPUs, the marketing
end of the company seems to never have realized their problems.
Early in the life of DEC when Ken Olsen was at the helm, DEC
did very well in a market that was dominated by companies who
sold cereal at $ 500/box.  While their computer systems could out
perform DEC by a margin of 5 to 1, they were obviously not cost
effective.

Initially, at the other end of the spectrum were the $ 2/box systems
which were only toys.  But the toys gradually grew into systems
that could do something useful.  Both IBM and DEC missed the
market - maybe in similar ways.  Since I am somewhat familiar
with DEC around the 1980s when most of the long term damage
was done, let me relate one obvious point.  The hardware and
software for a low cost system using a VT100 (actually a VT103)
and a cut down version of RT-11 were all in place in 1980.  One
distributor stated that he asked DEC to simply market the product
with a realistic price tag.  DECs answer was that they were not
interested in the retail mass-volume market.  DEC then proceeded
to inhibit their VARs to the point where many of the small ones
simply switched (with the 386 around 1985-88) to Intel.  If DEC
had considered that internal market suppression (favouring the
VAX over the PDP-11) often makes the problem worse
(IBM might have benifited from a voluntary separation), VAX
competition from the low side in the form of an independent
PDP-11 corporate structure might have produced a more
open DEC.  The basic difficulty from the user point of view
was that DEC simply could not compete with the third-party
hardware competition.  With the Qbus, however, the result
was a VAR work force and marketing arm which supplied
independent systems - often with only a DEC CPU - but
with with an ALL DEC operating system.  I once helped set
up the software environment for a PDP-11 that was to be
placed into a TANK - you know - big guns and all that.
Only a DEC CPU, but the OS was DEC.  When DEC
got sticky around 1985, the company switch to a 386.
No DEC CPU and especially no DEC OS.

What happened to the BIbus?  No third party products -
no useful life - a bit of an exageration - but contrast the
BIbus which was almost completely closed with the Qbus
which was almost completely open.

Or take a Micropolis 1325 => RD53:
     Approximately what factor was the price markup - ten?
     That would be adding a zero to the price - adding NOTHING
     What was the hardware alteration? A zero ohm resistor?
     That would be adding NOTHING on the hardware side.
     Add NOTHING twice to a Micropolis 1325 => RD53

While this last bit of analysis is supposed to be hunour, I do
remember reading about the "Corporate Battlewagon" in
Digital News and Review some time back under the Grimm
Fairy Tale section.

In short, I really feel that DEC had really great potential.
Ken Olsen basically seemed to allow the price to be based
on the potential rather than the cost of production and the
competition out in the market.  Those decisions may well
be what led DEC to its present great difficulties.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict





