From: dccoote@werple.mira.net.au (David Coote)
Subject: Digital Extended Mathematics Library (DXML).
Date: 1997/11/02
Message-ID: <63h0ts$r3b$1@eplet.mira.net.au>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 286533556
Organization: werple public-access Internet
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


We recently evaluated DXML under DEC UNIX 4.0b with ACT's GNAT Ada and 
Rational Apex Ada. Other than the usual joy of writing bindings for the DXML 
routines so they could be called from Ada programs this went really well. The 
FFT routines were roughly 50% faster than a hand-coded FFT and 20x - yes 20x - 
faster than an implementation out of a textbook. It looks like DEC have done a 
good job tuning at least the FFT implementation for the Alpha Architecture.

One trap for young players. The installation script checks the chip generation 
and installs appropriately. We're using EV56 4100's. The script seemed 
convinced that there are EV4 and EV5 generation chips out there and that's 
about it. So it wanted to do the EV4 installation when it didn't recognise 
EV56. DEC support tell me this is a known problem. Considering what they're 
charging for a DXML development license on a 4100 it would be nice if this was 
a known and fixed problem.

The other point about which neither DEC support or the dxml email address in 
the release notes has been very helpful is when there will be a DXML version 
for EV56 and EV6 chips. With something using the architecture as closely as 
does this package I would expect to see an improvement from using the new 
generation features. Again keeping in mind the cost of the package it would be 
nice to see DEC put out an EV56/EV6 DXML version.




