In article <636erd$m2l$1@ha1.rdc1.occa.home.com> "Merritt Family" <merritt1@home.com> writes:

>   Idiot!

Is that really necessary?

>   The Intel chip does not nor will it compete directly with the Alpha
>   chips.  The Intel chips are CISC whereas Alpha are RISC.  Also, Alpha will
>   be 128bit shorly after the Intel CISC 64bit hits market.

The fact that a one chip is CISC and another is RISC has nothing to do
with whether or not they are competitors.  If they're competing for
the same or at least overlapping markets, then they are competitors.
That said, it doesn't really apply to this situation anyway because
Merced is not CISC.  I'm not sure what they're really calling it, but
if anything, it's even more RISCy than Alpha (no pun intended),
inasmuch as the compiler is responsible for even more of the
optimization work.

Anders Trier Rasmussen wrote in message <3455DE5C.3F7F@dfs.dk>...

>After I haved been reading the settlement betwen Intel and DEC i belive,
>that the Alpha chip will be out of production after the Intel 64 bit
>chip as been shipped, because the settlement are saying, that DEC will
>port the DEC unix and NT/alpha to the new chip.

I don't follow this logic.  Porting an OS to a second architecture to
gain marketshare does not necessarily mean the first architecture will
be abandoned!  Do you expect Microsoft to abandon NT on IA-32 as soon
as they get it ported to IA-64?  Did Sun abandon Sparc as soon as they
ported Solaris to IA-32?  Digital has said nothing to imply that they
will drop support of Alpha.  In fact, they are saying that this
agreement with Intel makes it that much more possible to continue
producing and supporting Alpha for many years to come.  Why is that
incomprehensible? 

	Steve

