In article <1997Oct31.105644.1@eisner> Kilgallen@eisner.decus.org.nospam writes:
>There is nothing wrong with a 32 bit application if performance will
>not be enhanced by going to 64 bits.  

My specific quotes were for Unix systems. While it is true that only
relatively few applications need 64 bit memory addressing, the catch
is that you want a consistent API. When you file offsets are 32 bit
only, you *do* have a serious constraint. When your file offsets
are 64 bit, you start getting into consistency problems between various
standardized Unix interfaces. 

You can do it with "modes" (like SUN is planning and SGI has
implemented for years ) and #ifdef yourself to death, but I'd rather
have a simple programming model that just works.

