I've gotten a lot of responses from VMS users who are disgruntled with
the way VMS has been handled, and in response I can only say that
Digital's official position is that VMS support will continue. As to the
cost differential, the cost of Digital UNIX is also hugely higher than
than of NT. A cost of 10 or more is sometimes unavoidable. Remember, NT
is developed by Microsoft, and costs are amortized across millions of
licenses. VMS or Digital UNIX sells perhaps a couple of orders of
magnitude less in licenses. Given that, the software costs would
arguably need to be 100 or more times as high. 

On a different note, I've seen a lot of flames about how Digital has
nuked prior products, including the MIPS hardware. While painful for
customers (and Digital), it was something that had to be done.

Believe me, I understand the frustration of users who have had a vendor
drop support for a product. I understand it as a Digital field person,
and I understand it as a customer who was nailed by Sun and HP when at a
previous job. Digital is no different from any other manufacturer in
this regard. When a product becomes unprofitable, you move on, hopefully
offering your customer base an opportunity to migrate or upgrade. Lest
ye forget, Sun piped the Sun 3 for SPARC, and the 386i lived only what,
maybe a year? HP has done similar things for the 68k based machines with
PA RISC. They're about to do it to PA RISC with Merced. And they have
disgruntled customers as a result. At least Digital offered binary
translation technology to help ease the pain. You didn't see any other
vendor making the investments to do that. But the reality is that if an
architecture becomes unprofitable, you have to kill it. Otherwise you go
out of business. 

> The key, though, is that VMS needs to be MARKETED, and sales folks
> whose arms need to be twisted before they'll suggest it need to find
> other jobs, to be replaced by others who will...
You know, it's funny. I was working for Digital in 1990, and I heard
UNIX customers saying the same thing. Well, the simple truth is that
Digital sells VMS, UNIX, and NT. And to the average sales rep, a buck is
a buck, and they don't really care where they get it. Let the customer
decide. Isn't that what a vendor should do?

If you aren't getting support, that's bad. Digital employees should
support EVERY product that Digital sells. If you aren't getting the
support you need, that needs to be fixed. Call and complain. If you
can't find anybody else, send me mail. I can't guarantee results, but I
will try to find someone to resolve your problems.

> 
> So much for grump of the day...
> glenn
Wow, just one? Some people use up a month's quota in a day :-)

As always, the above represent my own opinions, and should in no way be
considered to represent an official statement by Digital Equipment
Corporation.

